29.09.2026  #ParticleAccelerators #ParticlePhysics #CERN
How do particle accelerators push tiny charged particles to almost the speed of light? In this slow and detailed journey, we follow the development of particle accelerators from the earliest experiments with charged particles to the enormous machines operating at CERN today.
We begin with the basic problem: how do you give a particle more and more energy while keeping its motion under control? Early experiments revealed three essential ingredients that would remain at the heart of accelerator physics: a source of particles, a way to accelerate them, and a way to control their trajectory.
From there, we follow the chain of problems and solutions that shaped accelerator technology. High-voltage machines such as the Cockcroft-Walton accelerator and Van de Graaff generator showed the possibilities and limitations of producing large electric potentials. Linear accelerators introduced repeated acceleration, while the cyclotron allowed particles to pass through the same accelerating region again and again.

As particles became faster, the physics became more demanding. Relativity changed the timing of the classical cyclotron, leading to the development of phase stability and the synchrocyclotron. Synchrotrons then synchronized the accelerating fields and magnetic fields with the increasing energy of the particles. Strong focusing made it possible to control beams much more precisely, while increasingly sophisticated magnets and beam dynamics allowed accelerators to grow larger and more powerful.

We then follow a proton through the modern CERN accelerator complex, from hydrogen and the linear accelerator through the Booster, Proton Synchrotron, Super Proton Synchrotron, and finally into the Large Hadron Collider. Along the way, we explore radiofrequency cavities, superconducting magnets, beam focusing, ultra-high vacuum, synchrotron radiation, particle bunches, and the reason collisions at higher energies allow physicists to investigate smaller structures and create new particles.

The story continues beyond the LHC. We look at antiprotons and antihydrogen, how particles can be slowed and trapped, and how accelerator technology has become useful far beyond fundamental physics.

Particle accelerators are used in medicine, including cancer treatment and PET imaging, as well as in industrial processing, materials research, sterilization, imaging, and neutron production.

The result is a long chain of engineering problems and physical solutions: source → acceleration → focusing → synchronization → collision → detection.

From a simple beam of electrons to the enormous accelerator complex beneath CERN, this is the story of how we learned to control charged particles at extraordinary energies.

Settle in, get comfortable, and explore particle accelerators slowly.

