1:02:551 Stunde, 2 Minuten, 55 Sekundenthe context was already doing. and the context might have been doing quite a lot. This is something that a Danish
1:03:031 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 3 Sekundenresearcher named Osborne Robartson who we talked about earlier, the one who challenged Beecher's original 35% claim,
1:03:121 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 12 Sekundenhas spent years thinking carefully about.
1:03:161 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 16 SekundenHerobiartson's position is nuanced and worth sitting with. He's skeptical of grand claims about placebo power and
1:03:241 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 24 Sekundenrightly so. He showed that when you compare placebo treatment against no treatment at all, the placebo effect is
1:03:321 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 32 Sekundenoften modest and mostly limited to subjective outcomes like pain and nausea rather than hard biological end points.
1:03:411 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 41 SekundenBut even doesn't deny that context matters. His point is more precise than that. His point is that we should be
1:03:501 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 50 Sekundencareful about what we attribute to placebo and what we attribute to other things.
1:03:561 Stunde, 3 Minuten, 56 Sekundenlike natural recovery, regression to the mean, the tendency for symptoms to improve on their own after
1:04:041 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 4 Sekundenyou seek help because you sought help at the moment they were worst.
1:04:101 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 10 SekundenThese are real statistical phenomena that inflate placebo response rates in trials and has been relentless about
1:04:181 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 18 Sekundenpointing them out. And he's right to be relentless because the distinction matters.
1:04:251 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 25 SekundenIf someone with a cold takes a sugar pill and feels better 4 days later, that's not the placebo effect. That's
1:04:331 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 33 Sekundenhaving an immune system. The cold was going to resolve anyway. The pill gets the credit, but the credit belongs to time and biology.
1:04:441 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 44 SekundenRegression to the mean is the same kind of trap. If you measure someone's pain on their worst day and then measure it
1:04:521 Stunde, 4 Minuten, 52 Sekundenagain a week later, it will probably be lower, not because anything you did helped, but because extreme values tend
1:05:001 Stunde, 5 Minutento drift back toward the average. This is basic statistics, not healing. And a
1:05:071 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 7 Sekundenlot of what gets called placebo response in clinical trials is really just these background forces doing their quiet,
1:05:151 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 15 Sekundenreliable work. So the honest picture is layered. When someone improves after receiving a placebo, some of that improvement is natural recovery.
1:05:271 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 27 SekundenSome of it is regression to the mean.
1:05:301 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 30 SekundenSome of it is reporting bias, the human tendency to tell the nice researcher that yes, you do feel a bit better
1:05:381 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 38 Sekundenbecause they've been so attentive. And some of it, a real and measurable sum of it, is genuine psychobiological
1:05:461 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 46 Sekundenresponse. Endorphins releasing, dopamine flowing, descending, pain modulation
1:05:531 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 53 Sekundenkicking in, the kinds of things Benadeti has documented with brain imaging and pharmacological blocking. The trick is
1:06:011 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 1 Sekundefiguring out how much of each, and that's extraordinarily difficult to do.
1:06:061 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 6 SekundenOne approach that researchers have used is the three arm trial. Instead of the usual two groups, drug versus placebo,
1:06:161 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 16 Sekundenyou add a third group that gets no treatment at all. They're on a waiting list or they receive standard care or
1:06:231 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 23 Sekundenthey simply come in for assessments without any intervention.
1:06:281 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 28 SekundenThis third group is your baseline. They show you what natural recovery and regression to the mean look like on
1:06:361 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 36 Sekundentheir own without any therapeutic context.
1:06:401 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 40 SekundenThen you can compare the placebo group against this no treatment group. And whatever difference you see that's closer to the true contextual effect.
1:06:501 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 50 SekundenIt's not perfect. Nothing in this field is perfect. But it gives you a much cleaner picture than the standard two
1:06:571 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 57 Sekundenarm design. And when researchers have done this, the results are interesting.
1:07:041 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 4 SekundenFor subjective outcomes, especially pain, the placebo group often does meaningfully better than the no
1:07:111 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 11 Sekundentreatment group. Not always, not for everything, but consistently enough that you can't wave it away. A Cochran
1:07:211 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 21 Sekundenreview, which is about as rigorous as evidence synthesis gets, found small to moderate effects of placebo
1:07:281 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 28 Sekundeninterventions on pain and nausea when compared against no treatment. Small to moderate, not miraculous, not 35% of
1:07:381 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 38 Sekundeneverything, the way Beecher claimed, but real and concentrated in the domains where the brain has the most direct influence over the experience.
1:07:481 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 48 SekundenWhich makes sense, right?
1:07:511 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 51 SekundenIf you think about what the placebo response actually is neurobiologically, it's the brain modulating its own
1:07:581 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 58 Sekundenoutputs. Pain perception, nausea, mood, fatigue.
1:08:041 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 4 SekundenThese are all experiences that the brain constructs and that the brain can within limits dial up or down. The brain is not
1:08:141 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 14 Sekundengoing to shrink a tumor because you expected it to. It's not going to knit a broken bone faster because the doctor was kind, but it can release endogenous
1:08:231 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 23 Sekundenopioids that change how much a broken bone hurts. It can adjust the neurochemical environment in ways that affect how nauseated you feel after chemotherapy.
1:08:331 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 33 SekundenIt can shift the balance of arousal and calm in ways that let you sleep or focus or stop clenching your jaw.
1:08:421 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 42 SekundenThese are real physiological changes with real consequences for how sick you feel and how well you function, even if they don't alter the underlying disease.
Kapitel 14: Depression and modern care
1:08:531 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 53 SekundenAnd this is where the conversation about integrating placebo insights into clinical practice gets genuinely
1:09:001 Stunde, 9 Minuteninteresting and genuinely tricky because the obvious conclusion, the one that practically writes itself, is that
1:09:081 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 8 Sekundendoctors should deliberately harness the placebo effect. They should be warmer, spend more time, create more ritual,
1:09:171 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 17 Sekundenoptimize the context. And at one level, yes, obviously. Capchuk's work on clinician warmth, Kelly's metaanalysis
1:09:261 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 26 Sekundenon therapeutic alliance, Olrich's study on hospital room windows, all of it points in the same direction. The
1:09:331 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 33 Sekundencontext matters, so make the context better. But the moment you try to operationalize that, you run into
1:09:411 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 41 Sekundenproblems that are not just logistical but genuinely ethical. The traditional placebo, the sugar pill given with a
1:09:491 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 49 Sekundenknowing wink, is deception. And deception violates informed consent, which is one of the foundational principles of modern medical ethics.
1:10:001 Stunde, 10 MinutenYou cannot lie to a patient about what you're giving them, even if the lie might help.
1:10:061 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 6 SekundenCapchuk's open label work sidesteps this beautifully and we talked about that earlier. The remarkable finding that
1:10:141 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 14 Sekundenplaceos can work even when people know they're placeos.
1:10:191 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 19 SekundenBut open label placeos are still a young field still being tested. And there are real questions about how durable those
1:10:271 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 27 Sekundeneffects are, how broadly they generalize and whether they would survive being scaled up from careful research settings
1:10:351 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 35 Sekundeninto the messy reality of a Tuesday afternoon clinic. There's also a deeper worry, one that a philosopher of
1:10:431 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 43 Sekundenmedicine named Jeremy Howick has written about thoughtfully. If we start optimizing the therapeutic context, if
1:10:511 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 51 Sekundenwe train doctors to perform warmth and certainty, are we turning medicine into manipulation?
1:11:001 Stunde, 11 MinutenHow doesn't think so, and his argument is interesting. He distinguishes between deceptive placeos, which involve lying,
1:11:081 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 8 Sekundenand what he calls honest place, or contextual healing, which involves simply being a good clinician.
1:11:171 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 17 Sekundenspending time with the patient, explaining the treatment clearly, expressing genuine care, creating an
1:11:251 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 25 Sekundenenvironment that supports healing. None of that is deception. It's just good medicine.
1:11:321 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 32 SekundenAnd the fact that it has measurable physiological effects doesn't make it manipulative any more than a surgeon washing their hands is manipulative
1:11:411 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 41 Sekundenbecause it reduces infection through a mechanism the patient doesn't fully understand.
1:11:481 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 48 SekundenBut there's a tension here that doesn't resolve neatly. And I think it's worth being honest about that because the same
1:11:561 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 56 Sekundenresearch that shows context matters also shows that more confident, more certain delivery produces stronger placebo
1:12:041 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 4 Sekundenresponses. A doctor who says this will definitely help generates more relief than one who says we'll try this and see. And that creates a genuine dilemma.
1:12:171 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 17 SekundenThe honest thing to say is often, "I'm not sure this will work for you." The therapeutically optimal thing to say
1:12:261 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 26 Sekundenmight be something more confident. And navigating that gap, being truthful without being deflating,
1:12:331 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 33 Sekundenbeing hopeful without being dishonest, that's an art, not a protocol.
1:12:401 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 40 SekundenIt can't be reduced to a checklist or a training module.
1:12:451 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 45 SekundenWhich brings us to something that the placebo literature has been circling for years without quite landing on.
1:12:531 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 53 SekundenSomething that connects the neuroscience and the ethics and the clinical reality into a single genuinely difficult
1:13:021 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 2 Sekundenquestion. And that question is about what happens when the placebo effect
1:13:081 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 8 Sekundenisn't a bonus, an added layer of healing on top of a real treatment. But when it's doing most of the work, when the
1:13:171 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 17 Sekundenspecific effect of the treatment is small or non-existent and the improvement the patient experiences is
1:13:251 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 25 Sekundencoming almost entirely from the context because that situation is not hypothetical.
1:13:331 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 33 SekundenIt describes a surprisingly large number of treatments in modern medicine. And it's worth sitting with that for a moment because the implications are wider than you might expect.
1:13:441 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 44 SekundenThink about anti-depressants.
1:13:461 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 46 SekundenThese are among the most prescribed medications in the developed world. Tens of millions of people take them. And the
1:13:541 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 54 Sekundenclinical trial data, when you look at it carefully, tells a story that is genuinely complicated.
1:14:021 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 2 SekundenIn 2008, Irving Kersh, a psychologist at Harvard, published a metaanalysis that included both published and unpublished
1:14:111 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 11 Sekundentrials of the most commonly prescribed anti-depressants, the SSRI, drugs like fluoxitine, which you
1:14:201 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 20 Sekundenprobably know as Prozac, and certillain, which is Zoloft, and a few others. What
1:14:261 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 26 SekundenKersh found and what made the study so controversial was that for most patients with mild to moderate depression, the
1:14:351 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 35 Sekundendifference between the drug and the placebo was clinically negligible. Both groups improved.
1:14:431 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 43 SekundenBoth groups improved quite a lot actually.
1:14:471 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 47 SekundenBut the drug group didn't improve much more than the placebo group.
1:14:521 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 52 SekundenThe gap was there statistically, but it was small enough that it fell below the threshold that regulatory agencies like
1:15:001 Stunde, 15 Minutenthe FDA and NICE in the UK had previously used to define a clinically meaningful difference. Now, this needs
1:15:101 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 10 Sekundencareful handling because it's one of those findings that can be stated in a way that's technically accurate but deeply misleading.
1:15:191 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 19 SekundenThe misleading version is anti-depressants don't work. That's not what Kersh found. What he found is that
1:15:281 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 28 Sekundenanti-depressants do work. People get better on them, but that a large portion of the improvement appears to come from
1:15:361 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 36 Sekundenthe placebo response rather than from the specific pharmacological action of the drug. For people with severe
1:15:441 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 44 Sekundendepression, the drug over placebo difference was larger and more clearly meaningful.
1:15:521 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 52 SekundenThe controversy is concentrated in the mild to moderate range, which is also where the vast majority of prescriptions
1:16:001 Stunde, 16 Minutenare written. And you've almost certainly heard this debate play out in some form because it became one of those findings
1:16:071 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 7 Sekundenthat escaped the journals and entered the culture. People started saying things like Prozac is just a placebo at
1:16:161 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 16 Sekundendinner parties, which is a spectacular oversimplification of what the data actually shows.
1:16:241 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 24 SekundenBut the data does show something important and uncomfortable which is that for a significant number of people taking anti-depressants.
1:16:321 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 32 SekundenThe ritual of treatment, the act of seeing a doctor, receiving a diagnosis,
1:16:401 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 40 Sekundenbeing given a pill, being told it will help, coming back for follow-up visits, having someone monitor your progress.
1:16:491 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 49 SekundenAll of that context is doing a lot of the therapeutic heavy lifting.
1:16:551 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 55 SekundenHere's what's strange about that. It doesn't mean those people aren't really getting better. They are getting better.
1:17:031 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 3 SekundenTheir symptoms improve. Their functioning improves. And the improvement is measurable.
1:17:101 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 10 SekundenIt's just that the mechanism driving the improvement may not be primarily the molecule. It may be the whole package.
1:17:181 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 18 Sekundenthe expectation, the care, the structure, the hope. And if you've been
1:17:251 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 25 Sekundenfollowing the thread of everything we've been talking about, that shouldn't surprise you at all because we've already seen that expectation and
1:17:331 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 33 Sekundencontext can trigger real endorphin release, real dopamine activity, real changes in how the brain processes pain
1:17:411 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 41 Sekundenand reward. There's no reason to think depression would be exempt from that.
1:17:461 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 46 SekundenThe brain systems involved in mood regulation are exactly the kind of systems that respond to top- down
1:17:531 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 53 Sekundenmodulation, to belief, to expectancy, to the feeling that something is being done. But think about what this means
1:18:011 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 1 Sekundepractically. If a doctor knows that the drug they're prescribing has a specific pharmacological effect that is small
1:18:091 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 9 Sekundenrelative to the contextual effect, what should they do? stop prescribing it.
1:18:161 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 16 SekundenThat would mean taking away the vehicle that delivers the context because you can't just hand someone a placebo and
1:18:231 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 23 Sekundensay, "Here, this will help your depression." Well, actually, Capchuk's open label
1:18:301 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 30 Sekundenwork suggests maybe you can a little, but the effect sizes there are modest and the evidence base is still thin. for
1:18:371 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 37 Sekundendepression specifically. The pill, even if its chemical contribution is small, serves as the anchor for the entire
1:18:451 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 45 Sekundentherapeutic ritual. It gives the patient something concrete to do every morning.
1:18:511 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 51 SekundenIt gives the doctor something to prescribe, to adjust, to follow up on. It creates a reason for regular contact.
1:18:591 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 59 SekundenIt generates hope. And all of that is genuinely therapeutic.
1:19:051 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 5 SekundenSo you end up in this strange place where the most rational response to the evidence might be to keep prescribing the drug, but to also invest much more
1:19:141 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 14 Sekundenseriously in the context around it, the length of the appointment, the quality of the explanation, the warmth of the
1:19:221 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 22 Sekundenfollowup, the sense that someone is paying attention. Because if context is doing half the work or more, then
1:19:301 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 30 Sekundenskimping on context is like prescribing half a dose. And this isn't just about anti-depressants.
1:19:361 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 36 SekundenA similar pattern shows up in treatments for chronic pain, for irritable bowel syndrome, for chronic fatigue, for certain anxiety disorders, for insomnia.
1:19:481 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 48 SekundenThese are conditions where the brain's own regulatory systems are deeply involved, where subjective experience is the primary outcome, and where the
1:19:571 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 57 Sekundenplacebo response tends to be large. In some chronic pain trials, the placebo response has been growing over time,
1:20:061 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 6 Sekundenparticularly in the United States, which is a genuinely puzzling finding.
1:20:111 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 11 SekundenJeffrey Mogul, a pain researcher at McGill University, published data in 2015 showing that the placebo response
1:20:191 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 19 Sekundenin American pain trials had increased significantly since the 1990s, while the drug response had stayed roughly the
1:20:261 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 26 Sekundensame. The result is that the gap between drug and placebo has been shrinking, making it harder and harder for new
1:20:331 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 33 Sekundenpainkillers to beat placebo in clinical trials.
1:20:371 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 37 SekundenNobody is entirely sure why this is happening, though there are some plausible theories. American clinical
1:20:441 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 44 Sekundentrials have become larger, longer, and more elaborate over the decades.
1:20:501 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 50 SekundenPatients in these trials receive more attention, more monitoring, more clinical contact. The trials themselves
1:20:581 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 58 Sekundenhave become better vehicles for delivering context. And if context drives the placebo response, then better
1:21:051 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 5 Sekundenresource trials would naturally produce bigger placebo effects. It's almost as if the machinery designed to test
1:21:121 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 12 Sekundenwhether drugs work has inadvertently become so good at delivering care that it's making the drugs look worse by comparison.
1:21:211 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 21 SekundenNow, you might be thinking that this is all a bit abstract, that it applies to researchers designing trials, but not to
1:21:291 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 29 Sekundensomeone lying in bed wondering how their own mind works. But here's the thing. If you've ever taken a medication and felt
1:21:381 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 38 Sekundenit start to work, felt the headache begin to ease 20 minutes after swallowing the pill, felt the anxiety
1:21:451 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 45 Sekundensoften an hour after your first dose of something new, part of what you were feeling was real pharmarmacology, and
1:21:541 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 54 Sekundenpart of it was your brain's own response to the act of taking the medication. And you can't separate those two things in
1:22:011 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 1 Sekundeyour own experience. They arrive blended together as a single feeling of getting better. Your brain doesn't label one
1:22:091 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 9 Sekundenstream this is the drug and another this is your expectation. It just produces relief. And the relief is real either
1:22:191 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 19 Sekundenway. Which brings up something that the placebo literature bumps into repeatedly but rarely says out loud. And it has to
1:22:271 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 27 Sekundendo with how we think about what counts as real medicine.
1:22:311 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 31 SekundenBecause our intuitive model of medicine is basically mechanical.
1:22:361 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 36 SekundenYou have a problem, the doctor identifies it, gives you a substance or a procedure that fixes it, and you get
1:22:441 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 44 Sekundenbetter because of what the substance or procedure does to your body.
1:22:491 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 49 SekundenAnd that model works beautifully for a lot of medicine. Antibiotics kill bacteria.
1:22:561 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 56 SekundenInsulin replaces a hormone your body isn't making. surgery removes a tumor.
1:23:021 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 2 SekundenThe mechanism is specific, the effect is direct, and the context around the delivery is mostly irrelevant. You could
1:23:101 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 10 Sekundentake the antibiotic without knowing what it is, and it would still kill the bacteria.
1:23:161 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 16 SekundenBut for a large and arguably growing category of conditions, the ones that involve the brain's own regulatory
1:23:231 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 23 Sekundensystems, the ones where subjective experience is the thing being treated, that mechanical model is incomplete.
1:23:311 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 31 SekundenNot wrong, but incomplete.
1:23:341 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 34 SekundenBecause for those conditions, the context isn't just wrapping paper around the real treatment. It's part of the
1:23:411 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 41 Sekundentreatment. the therapeutic relationship, the explanation, the expectation, the
1:23:481 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 48 Sekundenritual, the environment. These aren't soft extras.
1:23:541 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 54 SekundenThey're active ingredients. And the evidence for that is not handwavy or philosophical.
1:24:001 Stunde, 24 MinutenIt's the nlloxxone reversible endorphin release that Benedetti measured. It's the dopamine activity in the nucleus encumbent that shows up on PET scans.
1:24:101 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 10 SekundenIt's the conditioned immune responses that Ader demonstrated in rats. It's the open hidden infusion studies showing
1:24:181 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 18 Sekundenthat the same drug at the same dose produces measurably less effect when the patient doesn't know they're receiving
1:24:241 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 24 Sekundenit. So the question that the placebo effect keeps posing, the one that makes it so persistently confusing, is whether
1:24:331 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 33 Sekundenmedicine needs a wider definition of what counts as a real treatment.
1:24:381 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 38 SekundenNot wider in the sense of accepting anything that makes someone feel better, regardless of evidence. Wider in the
1:24:451 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 45 Sekundensense of recognizing that evidence-based context, the kind that has been shown to produce measurable physiological
1:24:511 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 51 Sekundenchanges, deserves the same serious attention that we give to molecules and procedures.
1:24:591 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 59 SekundenAnd some people are starting to build that case quite carefully.
1:25:031 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 3 SekundenTed Capchuk at Harvard has argued for years that what we call the placebo effect should really be understood as a
1:25:121 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 12 Sekundencollection of context dependent neurobiological responses that can be studied, measured, and in some cases
1:25:211 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 21 Sekundendeliberately enhanced without any deception at all.
1:25:251 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 25 SekundenFabitio Benadet in Turin has mapped specific neural pathways through which expectation and conditioning produce
1:25:331 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 33 Sekundentheir effects and he's been careful to distinguish between conditions where these mechanisms are robust and
1:25:401 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 40 Sekundenconditions where they're minimal or absent.
1:25:441 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 44 SekundenThese aren't fringe figures. They're publishing in the top journals and the field is slowly cautiously beginning to absorb what they're saying.
1:25:561 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 56 SekundenBut slowly is the operative word because the institutional structures of medicine, the way trials are designed,
1:26:051 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 5 Sekundenthe way drugs are approved, the way doctors are trained, the way appointments are scheduled and reimbursed.
1:26:141 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 14 SekundenAll of those structures were built around the mechanical model. They were built to test molecules and procedures, not to optimize context.
1:26:251 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 25 SekundenAnd changing those structures is an enormously difficult thing. Not because anyone disagrees in principle that being
1:26:321 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 32 Sekundenkind to patience is good, but because being kind to patience is hard to standardize, hard to measure, hard to bill for, and hard to scale.
1:26:441 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 44 SekundenThe 15-minute appointment exists for economic reasons, not because anyone thinks it's the ideal amount of time to spend with a person who is suffering.
1:26:531 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 53 SekundenAnd so, the placebo effect sits in this odd position in modern medicine. Everyone acknowledges it exists.
1:27:011 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 1 SekundeEveryone controls for it in trials, but almost nobody is systematically trying to harness it to build clinical systems
1:27:101 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 10 Sekundenthat deliberately maximize the contextual factors that produce real neurobiological responses in patients.
1:27:181 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 18 SekundenIt's a bit like knowing that sunlight helps plants grow and then designing all your green houses with no windows
1:27:271 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 27 Sekundenbecause the important thing is the fertilizer.
Kapitel 15: The nocebo effect
1:27:301 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 30 SekundenThere's one more layer to this that I think is worth exploring before we start to pull the threads together. And it has to do with something we haven't really
1:27:381 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 38 Sekundentouched on yet, which is what happens when the placebo effect goes in the other direction. Because everything we've been talking about, the
1:27:471 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 47 Sekundenexpectation of benefit producing real physiological improvement has a mirror image. And that mirror image is called
1:27:561 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 56 Sekundenthe noibo effect. It's what happens when negative expectations produce real
1:28:041 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 4 Sekundenphysiological harm. And the evidence for it is, if anything, even more unsettling
1:28:111 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 11 Sekundenthan the evidence for placebo, because it suggests that the same machinery the mind uses to heal itself can, under the
1:28:191 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 19 Sekundenright conditions, make you genuinely sick. Let's start with something concrete.
1:28:261 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 26 SekundenIn the early 2000s, a young man in his 20s was admitted to a hospital in the American South after swallowing an
1:28:341 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 34 Sekundenentire bottle of pills. He was pale, trembling, his blood pressure had dropped dangerously low, and he told the
1:28:421 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 42 Sekundenemergency room doctors that he had tried to end his life by overdosing on anti-depressants.
1:28:481 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 48 SekundenThey treated him aggressively, introvenous fluids, monitoring, the full protocol for a serious overdose. And for
1:28:571 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 57 Sekundenseveral hours his condition remained precarious. His blood pressure stayed low. His heart rate was erratic. He was
1:29:061 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 6 Sekundenby every clinical measure a man whose body was responding to a toxic dose of medication.
1:29:131 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 13 SekundenThen his doctor from the clinical trial he'd been enrolled in arrived at the hospital and explained something. The pills he'd swallowed weren't anti-depressants.
1:29:241 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 24 SekundenThey were placeos. sugar pills.
1:29:281 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 28 SekundenHe'd been in the control arm of the trial the entire time. There was nothing pharmacologically active in his system.
1:29:361 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 36 SekundenNothing at all.
1:29:381 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 38 SekundenWithin 15 minutes of learning this, the man's blood pressure returned to normal.
1:29:431 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 43 SekundenHis symptoms resolved completely. He was fine. This case was reported by a
1:29:491 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 49 Sekundenresearcher named Roy Reeves in 2007 and it sits in the literature as one of the most vivid illustrations of what the
1:29:581 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 58 Sekundennoibo effect can do. This man's body produced a genuine measurable dangerous
1:30:051 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 5 Sekundenphysiological response to an overdose of nothing because he believed the pills were real and he believed he had taken a
1:30:121 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 12 Sekundenlethal dose. His expectation of harm created the harm and his expectation dissolving the moment he learned the truth dissolved the harm along with it.
1:30:261 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 26 SekundenNow you might be thinking that's an extreme case, a dramatic oneoff. And it is dramatic. But the noibo effect shows
1:30:351 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 35 Sekundenup in far more ordinary settings. And that's where it gets genuinely important for understanding how medicine works in practice.
1:30:451 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 45 SekundenThink about side effects.
1:30:471 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 47 SekundenWhen you're given a new medication, you're usually told what the possible side effects are. That's informed consent, and it's ethically necessary.
1:30:581 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 58 SekundenBut here's the thing. A significant proportion of the side effects people experience on medication are not caused
1:31:061 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 6 Sekundenby the drug itself. They're caused by the expectation of side effects. Fabitio
1:31:131 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 13 SekundenBenadeti, the same researcher whose work on endorphins and dopamine we talked about earlier has studied this extensively.
1:31:221 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 22 SekundenIn one line of research, he and his colleagues looked at what happens when you tell patients about a specific side effect before giving them a treatment.
1:31:331 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 33 SekundenThe patients who were warned about a particular side effect were significantly more likely to experience that exact side effect even when the
1:31:421 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 42 Sekundentreatment they received was pharmacologically inert.
1:31:471 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 47 SekundenThis isn't people making things up. This isn't hypocchondria or attention seeking. The noibo response involves real measurable physiological changes.
1:31:581 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 58 SekundenWhen someone expects pain, their brain can activate colicystokin pathways which are genuinely pronosceptive meaning they amplify pain signals.
1:32:101 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 10 SekundenBenadeti showed that you could block noiboinduced pain with proglamid a cholicy antagonist just as you could block placebo analesia with nlloxxone.
1:32:221 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 22 SekundenThe symmetry is almost elegant. Positive expectations recruit endorphins and reduce pain. Negative expectations
1:32:311 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 31 Sekundenrecruit callenan and increase it. Same machinery opposite direction.
1:32:381 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 38 SekundenAnd this has enormous practical implications because it means that the way a doctor communicates side effects can literally change whether those side effects occur.
1:32:511 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 51 SekundenNot just whether the patient notices them or reports them, but whether the body produces them.
1:32:591 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 59 SekundenA study published in 2012 by Winfried Reef and colleagues at the University of Marberg looked at statin medications,
1:33:081 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 8 Sekundenthe cholesterol lowering drugs that millions of people take.
1:33:131 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 13 SekundenThey found that patients who were told to expect muscle pain from statins were significantly more likely to report
1:33:201 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 20 Sekundenmuscle pain and in some cases to discontinue the medication entirely compared to patients who were given the same drug without that specific warning.
1:33:301 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 30 SekundenThe muscle pain was real. The patients genuinely felt it. But it wasn't being generated by the chemical properties of
1:33:391 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 39 Sekundenthe statin. It was being generated by the expectation that the statin would cause muscle pain. And you've almost
1:33:461 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 46 Sekundencertainly encountered some version of this yourself.
1:33:501 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 50 SekundenYou read the leaflet that comes with a medication, the one with the long list of possible side effects printed in tiny
1:33:581 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 58 Sekundentype and within a day or two you start noticing one of them. a slight headache, a bit of nausea, a funny feeling in your
1:34:071 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 7 Sekundenstomach, and you can never quite be sure whether the drug is causing it or whether you're causing it by expecting
1:34:141 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 14 Sekundenit. The honest answer is that in many cases, it's both or it's impossible to
1:34:211 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 21 Sekundenseparate the two because the expectation and the pharmarmacology are running through some of the same neural systems.
1:34:291 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 29 SekundenThis creates a genuine ethical problem and it's worth sitting with for a moment.
1:34:361 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 36 SekundenInformed consent requires that doctors tell patients about potential side effects. That's a bedrock principle of
1:34:441 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 44 Sekundenmodern medical ethics and for good reason. People have a right to know what a treatment might do to them. But the
1:34:521 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 52 Sekundenact of informing them about side effects can itself produce those side effects through the noibo mechanism.
1:35:011 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 1 SekundeSo the doctor is caught in a bind. Tell the patient everything and you risk making them sicker through negative expectation.
1:35:101 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 10 SekundenDon't tell them and you violate their autonomy and their right to make informed decisions about their own care.
1:35:171 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 17 SekundenThere's no clean solution to this, but some researchers have proposed what they call contextualized informed consent,
1:35:251 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 25 Sekundenwhere instead of simply listing every possible side effect, the doctor frames the information in a way that minimizes nobo activation.
1:35:341 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 34 SekundenSomething like most people tolerate this medication very well, and in clinical trials, the vast majority experienced no
1:35:421 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 42 Sekundensignificant side effects. A small number of people reported headaches, but this was also true of people taking the placebo.
1:35:521 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 52 SekundenThat framing is still honest. It still discloses the side effect, but it embeds the disclosure in a context of
1:35:591 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 59 Sekundenreassurance rather than a context of warning. And the evidence suggests that this framing genuinely reduces the incidence of noibo-driven side effects.
1:36:101 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 10 SekundenWhich brings us back to something we keep circling. This idea that context is pharmacologically active.
1:36:171 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 17 SekundenThe nibo effect is maybe the starkkest demonstration of that principle. The words a doctor uses, the tone of voice,
1:36:251 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 25 Sekundenthe facial expression, the pamphlet in the waiting room. All of these are not just window dressing around the real
1:36:341 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 34 Sekundentreatment. They are inputs to the same predictive machinery in the brain that generates both placebo and noibo
1:36:421 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 42 Sekundenresponses. And that machinery doesn't distinguish between a molecule entering the bloodstream and a sentence entering
1:36:501 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 50 Sekundenthe ear. It processes both as information about what is likely to happen next and it adjusts the body's physiology accordingly.
1:36:591 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 59 SekundenThere's a particularly striking study that illustrates this from the domain of allergies. In the late 1990s, Japanese
1:37:071 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 7 Sekundenresearchers led by Akimei and Nakagawa conducted an experiment with a group of boys who were highly allergic to a lacquer tree, similar to poison ivy.
1:37:171 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 17 SekundenThey blindfolded the boys and rubbed one arm with leaves from the lacquer tree while telling them it was a harmless chestnut tree. On the other arm, they
1:37:261 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 26 Sekundenrubbed harmless chestnut tree leaves while telling them it was the lacquer tree. The results were remarkable.
1:37:341 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 34 SekundenMany of the boys showed no allergic reaction on the arm touched by the actual allergen because they believed it was harmless. But they developed genuine
1:37:441 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 44 Sekundenallergic reactions, redness, itching, swelling on the arm touched by the harmless leaves because they believed those leaves were the allergen.
1:37:551 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 55 SekundenNow, this is an older study and a small one, and it should be held with the appropriate caution, but it points to
1:38:031 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 3 Sekundensomething that more recent and more rigorous work has confirmed, which is that the immune system itself is not
1:38:111 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 11 Sekundenoperating independently of the brain's expectations.
1:38:161 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 16 SekundenRobert Ader's work on conditioned immunosuppression which we talked about much earlier showed that the immune system could be
1:38:241 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 24 Sekundentrained like a Pavlovian response. The noibo research extends that finding into the territory of harm. If the brain
1:38:331 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 33 Sekundenexpects an immune threat, it can mount an immune response even when no threat is present. And this is where the noibo
1:38:411 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 41 Sekundeneffect starts to matter for understanding some genuinely puzzling medical phenomena.
1:38:471 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 47 SekundenTake the question of why some people develop severe debilitating side effects from treatments that should not
1:38:551 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 55 Sekundenpharmarmacologically be capable of producing those effects or why some patients deteriorate rapidly after
1:39:021 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 2 Sekundenreceiving a serious diagnosis faster than the disease itself would predict.
1:39:081 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 8 SekundenThere's a concept in medical anthropology called voodoo death. A term coined by the physiologist Walter Cannon
1:39:161 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 16 Sekundenway back in 1942 to describe cases where people in traditional societies apparently died after being cursed or hexed by a shaman.
1:39:271 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 27 SekundenCanon proposed that the mechanism was essentially a massive sustained stress response. that the person's absolute
1:39:351 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 35 Sekundenbelief in the curse triggered a catastrophic activation of the sympathetic nervous system that eventually caused cardiac failure.
1:39:451 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 45 SekundenNow, Canon's specific physiological explanation has been debated and refined over the decades and the original case
1:39:531 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 53 Sekundenreports he drew on are ethnographically messy. But the underlying principle that a person's belief about what is
1:40:021 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 2 Sekundenhappening to their body can produce physiological changes severe enough to be dangerous is not fringe science.
1:40:111 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 11 SekundenIt is consistent with everything we know about how the brain regulates the autonomic nervous system, the immune system, and the endocrine system. The
1:40:201 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 20 Sekundennoibo effect is not magic. It is the same predictive processing we've been talking about all evening. the brain's
1:40:281 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 28 Sekundenconstant project of anticipating what the body will need and adjusting physiology in advance.
1:40:351 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 35 SekundenExcept now the prediction is a harmful one and the adjustment goes in the wrong direction.
1:40:431 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 43 SekundenAnd here's what's genuinely interesting about this for the bigger picture. The noibo effect makes it very difficult to
1:40:511 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 51 Sekundenmaintain the position that the placebo effect is just psychological as if psychological means not real, not physical, not important.
1:41:021 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 2 SekundenBecause if negative expectations can produce allergic reactions, amplify pain through specific neurochemical pathways,
1:41:111 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 11 Sekundentank blood pressure, and potentially contribute to cardiac events.
1:41:151 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 15 Sekundenthen we are clearly talking about a mindbody interaction that is as physiologically real as any drug effect.
1:41:231 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 23 SekundenThe word just has no business being in that sentence. So when we pull back and look at the full landscape of what we've
1:41:311 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 31 Sekundenbeen talking about tonight, what emerges is something like this. The brain is a prediction engine. It is constantly
1:41:401 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 40 Sekundengenerating forecasts about what will happen next and it adjusts the body's internal state to match those forecasts.
1:41:481 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 48 SekundenWhen the prediction is positive, when the context signals safety, care, effective treatment, the body shifts
1:41:571 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 57 Sekundentoward healing. When the prediction is negative, when the context signals danger, harm worsening, the body shifts toward defense and distress.
1:42:091 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 9 SekundenBoth of these shifts are real. Both involve specific identifiable neurobiological mechanisms. Both can be
1:42:171 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 17 Sekundenmeasured, blocked, and in some cases deliberately shaped. And that means that every medical encounter, every
1:42:251 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 25 Sekundeninteraction between a person who is suffering and a person who is trying to help is an opportunity to move the dial
1:42:321 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 32 Sekundenin one direction or the other. Not through deception, not through wishful thinking, but through the careful
1:42:401 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 40 Sekundenevidence-based management of context, expectation, and the therapeutic relationship itself, which is a hopeful
1:42:491 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 49 Sekundenthought, actually, and one that the field is slowly beginning to act on. But there's still a question we haven't
Kapitel 16: Mind-body integration
1:42:561 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 56 Sekundenquite addressed. One that sits underneath everything we've covered. And it has to do with what all of this tells
1:43:041 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 4 Sekundenus about the nature of the boundary between mind and body in the first place. Because the placebo effect
1:43:121 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 12 Sekundendoesn't just challenge how we do medicine. It challenges something deeper. Something about how we think about what a human being actually is.
1:43:231 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 23 SekundenAnd that's the question worth sitting with here at the end of all this because it turns out to be one of the oldest
1:43:321 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 32 Sekundenquestions in the entire history of human thought and the placebo effect lands right in the middle of it in a way that
1:43:391 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 39 Sekundennobody quite expected. For most of the history of western medicine and really for most of the history of western
1:43:471 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 47 Sekundenphilosophy, the assumption has been that the mind and the body are two fundamentally different kinds of thing.
1:43:541 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 54 SekundenYou can trace this back at least to Daycart in the 1600s who drew a very clean line between the thinking
1:44:011 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 1 Sekundesubstance the resetans and the physical substance the resensa and said they were made of different stuff entirely.
1:44:111 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 11 SekundenThe body was a machine. The mind was something else, something immaterial, something that observed and directed the machine but was not itself mechanical.
1:44:221 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 22 SekundenAnd even though almost no working scientist today would describe themselves as a cartisian dualist, the architecture of that idea is still
1:44:311 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 31 Sekundenquietly embedded in the way we organize medicine, the way we talk about illness, and the way we think about treatment.
1:44:391 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 39 SekundenWe have doctors for the body and therapists for the mind.
1:44:441 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 44 SekundenWe have real diseases, meaning ones with clear physical pathology.
1:44:501 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 50 SekundenAnd then we have conditions that are psychological, which in practice often means less real, less serious, less
1:44:581 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 58 Sekundendeserving of the same kind of rigorous attention. The phrase, "It's all in your head," is still in many medical contexts
1:45:061 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 6 Sekundena polite way of saying it isn't really happening. And what the placebo effect does quietly and persistently is make that division impossible to maintain.
1:45:191 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 19 SekundenThink about what we've actually been describing tonight. A sugar pill triggers the release of endogenous opioids in the brain stem and those
1:45:261 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 26 Sekundenopioids reduce pain through the same receptor pathways that morphine uses. A sham injection activates dopamine
1:45:341 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 34 Sekundencircuits in the nucleus cumbent in Parkinson's patients and their motor symptoms temporarily improve. A person
1:45:411 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 41 Sekundentold that a harmless leaf is poison ivy develops real blisters with real histamine involvement, real immune activation.
1:45:511 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 51 SekundenA person conditioned with an immunosuppressive drug continues to show suppressed immune function when given a placebo weeks later with no drug in their system.
1:46:021 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 2 SekundenA person who believes they have overdosed on a medication they were told was an anti-depressant, but which was actually an inert capsule
1:46:111 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 11 Sekundenshows up in an emergency room with dangerously low blood pressure and a heart rate of 110.
1:46:181 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 18 SekundenAnd those vital signs are real and they resolve only when the deception is revealed. None of these things should be
1:46:251 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 25 Sekundenpossible if the mind and the body are truly separate systems.
1:46:311 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 31 SekundenIf thoughts are one kind of thing and biochemistry is another, there is no pathway by which believing something could change your immune function, your
1:46:391 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 39 Sekundendopamine levels, your blood pressure, or your experience of pain.
1:46:451 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 45 SekundenThe fact that it does reliably, measurably through identifiable neurochemical mechanisms means that the
1:46:521 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 52 Sekundenboundary we drew between the mental and the physical is not where we thought it was. Or maybe it means there is no
1:46:591 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 59 Sekundenboundary at all, just different levels of description of the same integrated system. This is not a mystical claim.
1:47:071 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 7 SekundenIt's actually the opposite. It's the recognition that when we say the mind did that, what we really mean is that a
1:47:151 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 15 Sekundenparticular pattern of neural activity shaped by learning, expectation, context, and social cues cascaded into
1:47:241 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 24 Sekundenendocrine changes, immune modulation, autonomic adjustments, and altered sensory processing. All through physical
1:47:331 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 33 Sekundenpathways that we can trace and in some cases interrupt with specific drugs.
1:47:391 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 39 SekundenThe mind is not a ghost hovering above the body and occasionally reaching down to interfere. The mind is what the body
1:47:471 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 47 Sekundenis doing at a certain level of organization. Beliefs are implemented in neurons.
1:47:531 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 53 SekundenExpectations are patterns of predictive firing. Hope is a neurochemical event.
1:47:591 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 59 SekundenAnd when a doctor sits with a patient and listens carefully and explains a treatment with warmth and confidence, that interaction is not merely psychological.
1:48:101 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 10 SekundenIt is a physiological intervention delivered through the sensory systems processed by cortical and subcortical
1:48:171 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 17 Sekundencircuits and transduced into measurable changes in the body's internal state.
1:48:231 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 23 SekundenWhich means, and here's the part that matters for the future of medicine, that context is not a confound to be
1:48:301 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 30 Sekundencontrolled away. It is an active ingredient. It has dose response characteristics.
1:48:371 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 37 SekundenIt has specific mechanisms. It can be enhanced or diminished. And the growing edge of placebo research is precisely
1:48:461 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 46 Sekundenabout figuring out how to use that ingredient deliberately, ethically, and well.
1:48:541 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 54 SekundenThere are people working on this right now.
1:48:571 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 57 SekundenCapchuk's group at Harvard, the one that ran those open label placebo trials, is continuing to explore how you can
1:49:051 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 5 Sekundenharness expectation effects without deception, which was always the ethical sticking point. Fabitio Benadet's lab in
1:49:141 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 14 SekundenTrin is mapping the specific neural circuits involved with increasing precision, trying to understand why some
1:49:231 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 23 Sekundenpeople respond more than others and whether you can predict who will benefit most from context enhanced care.
1:49:311 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 31 SekundenThere are researchers looking at how to train clinicians not just in pharmarmacology and procedure but in the deliberate skilled management of
1:49:401 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 40 Sekundentherapeutic context. The words they use, the way they frame a diagnosis, the quality of attention they bring to
1:49:491 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 49 Sekundenan encounter, not as a replacement for effective treatments, but as something that amplifies them, that gets more
1:49:571 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 57 Sekundenhealing out of the same intervention by attending to the full picture of what a treatment actually is.
1:50:041 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 4 SekundenAnd there are harder questions, too, ones that the field hasn't resolved.
1:50:091 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 9 SekundenIf the placebo response varies by culture, by individual, by condition, by the color of the pill, and the warmth of
1:50:181 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 18 Sekundenthe practitioner, how do you standardize it?
1:50:231 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 23 SekundenHow do you build it into clinical trials in a way that's rigorous?
1:50:271 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 27 SekundenHow do you train for it without turning it into a performance, something fake and scripted that patients see right through? Because
1:50:361 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 36 Sekundenremember, the therapeutic relationship works precisely because it's genuine.
1:50:421 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 42 SekundenYou can't manufacture trust. You can't fake presence.
1:50:471 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 47 SekundenThe mechanism runs on authenticity, which makes it a strange kind of tool, one that stops working the moment it
1:50:541 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 54 Sekundenbecomes purely instrumental. And there is still the question of limits. The placebo effect is real, but it is not
1:51:031 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 3 Sekundenmagic. It does not shrink tumors. It does not set broken bones. It does not cure infections.
1:51:101 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 10 SekundenWhat it does and does reliably is modulate the body's own regulatory systems. The ones that manage pain,
1:51:181 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 18 Sekundeninflammation, motor function, mood, immune vigilance, and autonomic balance.
1:51:251 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 25 SekundenThese are not small things. These are the systems that determine how a person feels and functions on a daily basis.
1:51:341 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 34 SekundenAnd for many chronic conditions, they are the primary battleground. But they are not everything.
1:51:411 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 41 SekundenThe honest version of the placebo story is that the mind's influence on the body is genuine, specific, and far more
1:51:501 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 50 Sekundenextensive than the old dismissive model allowed. and also that it operates within constraints set by the underlying biology.
1:52:001 Stunde, 52 MinutenBoth of those things are true at the same time. And holding them together without collapsing into either it's all
1:52:071 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 7 Sekundenin your head or you can think yourself well is the mature position, the one the
1:52:131 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 13 Sekundenevidence actually supports. So that's the landscape. That's what we've been walking through tonight. From Henry
1:52:211 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 21 SekundenBeecher's battlefield observation to open label placeos. From Benadet's nlloxxone experiments to the strange
