1:05:561 Stunde, 5 Minuten, 56 SekundenThink about international diplomacy where negotiations often happen in a second language for at least some participants.
1:06:051 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 5 SekundenThink about therapy.
1:06:081 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 8 SekundenThere's a whole literature on whether bilingual patients should do therapy in their first or second language. And the
1:06:171 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 17 Sekundenanswer often depends on what kind of emotional access the therapist is trying to facilitate.
1:06:241 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 24 SekundenFirst language for deep emotional processing. Second language when the patient needs some distance from overwhelming feelings. The language
1:06:331 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 33 Sekundenisn't just a communication tool. It's a cognitive setting that adjusts the emotional thermostat.
Kapitel 14: Language absence and growth
1:06:421 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 42 SekundenBut let me push this even further because there's another line of research that I find genuinely mindbending. And
1:06:491 Stunde, 6 Minuten, 49 Sekundenit has to do with what happens not just when you learn a second language, but when you lose one or when you have two languages competing in real time.
1:07:011 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 1 SekundeThere's a phenomenon called language attrition.
1:07:041 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 4 SekundenIf you grow up speaking one language and then move to a country where you speak a different language for decades, your first language doesn't just sit there
1:07:131 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 13 Sekundenunchanged in your head like a book on a shelf. It starts to erode. Vocabulary fades. Grammar gets rusty.
1:07:231 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 23 SekundenAnd here's what's wild. The cognitive patterns associated with that first language can fade, too.
1:07:311 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 31 SekundenMonica Schmid at the University of York has done extensive work on this, studying German Jews who immigrated
1:07:381 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 38 Sekundenduring the Nazi period and spent the rest of their lives speaking English or Hebrew or Portuguese.
1:07:461 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 46 SekundenDecades later, their German showed varying degrees of attrition, and the degree of attrition correlated with
1:07:531 Stunde, 7 Minuten, 53 Sekundenemotional factors. Those who had the most traumatic associations with their German-speaking past showed more
1:08:011 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 1 Sekundeattrition, as if the mind was actively letting go of the language and everything that came with it. And on the
1:08:091 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 9 Sekundenflip side, there's code switching, the fluid alternation between languages that natural bilingual do all the time. If
1:08:191 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 19 Sekundenyou've ever been around someone who mixes, say, Spanish and English in a single conversation, you know it's not random. Code switching follows
1:08:271 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 27 Sekundensophisticated grammatical rules, and it serves communicative and cognitive functions.
1:08:341 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 34 SekundenSometimes you switch because the other language has the better word for what you're feeling. Sometimes you switch
1:08:421 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 42 Sekundenbecause the other language has the right cognitive frame. A concept that's clunky in one language might be elegant in
1:08:491 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 49 Sekundenanother. And bilingual instinctively reach for the tool that fits. This brings us to something that the linguist
1:08:571 Stunde, 8 Minuten, 57 SekundenFrancois Grojan emphasized throughout his career. The idea that a bilingual is
1:09:031 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 3 Sekundennot two monolingual in one body. A bilingual is a unique cognitive system.
1:09:101 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 10 SekundenThe two languages interact, influence each other, create something that neither language alone would produce.
1:09:171 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 17 SekundenAnd this has been supported by neuroiming work showing that both languages are active in a bilingual's brain, even when they're ostensibly
1:09:261 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 26 Sekundenusing just one. The other language is always there, humming in the background, exerting subtle influence. So, what does
1:09:361 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 36 Sekundenall this add up to? I think it points toward a picture of human cognition that's far more flexible and far more
1:09:441 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 44 Sekundenlanguage sensitive than the strong universalist position would suggest, but also far more resilient and creative than the strong position implied.
1:09:551 Stunde, 9 Minuten, 55 SekundenYou're not trapped by your language, but you're not independent of it either.
1:10:001 Stunde, 10 MinutenYou're in a dance with it. And if you learn new languages, you learn new dances.
1:10:071 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 7 SekundenNow, around the same time that bilingualism research was producing these insights, another group of
1:10:141 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 14 Sekundenresearchers was approaching the language and thought question from a completely different angle. One that would prove just as illuminating.
1:10:241 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 24 SekundenInstead of asking what happens when you add a language, they asked what happens when language is absent.
1:10:321 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 32 SekundenWhat can we learn from people who for one reason or another grew up without full access to language?
1:10:401 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 40 SekundenAnd this takes us into some of the most poignant and revealing case studies in the entire field because it turns out that studying what
1:10:491 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 49 Sekundenhappens without language tells you an enormous amount about what language actually does.
1:10:551 Stunde, 10 Minuten, 55 SekundenThere's a researcher named Susan Golden Meadow at the University of Chicago who has spent decades studying deaf children who are not exposed to sign language.
1:11:061 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 6 SekundenChildren born to hearing parents who don't sign in communities without a deaf school. Children who essentially have no
1:11:141 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 14 Sekundenlinguistic input. And what these children do is remarkable. They invent their own gesture systems.
1:11:231 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 23 SekundenThese aren't random flailing or simple pointing. They're structured communication systems with consistent
1:11:291 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 29 Sekundenvocabulary with rudimentary grammar with ways of combining gestures to express novel ideas.
1:11:381 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 38 SekundenGolden Meadow calls these children home signers. And what they create all on
1:11:451 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 45 Sekundentheir own without any model to copy is genuinely astonishing.
1:11:521 Stunde, 11 Minuten, 52 SekundenThink about what that means for a second. A child maybe four or 5 years old with no language coming in, no
1:12:001 Stunde, 12 Minutenspoken words they can hear, no signs anyone is teaching them, and they spontaneously begin building a
1:12:081 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 8 Sekundencommunication system from scratch. They develop consistent gestures for objects and actions.
1:12:151 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 15 SekundenThey develop ordering rules like putting the object gesture before the action gesture.
1:12:211 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 21 SekundenThey even develop ways of modifying gestures to indicate things like number or size. It's not a full language. It
1:12:301 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 30 Sekundenlacks the complexity and depth of any natural sign language. But it's structured. It's productive. And it emerges without instruction.
1:12:411 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 41 SekundenAnd here's what's really fascinating about Golden Meadows work. She didn't just document what these children could
1:12:491 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 49 Sekundencommunicate. She studied what they could think. She gave home signing children cognitive tasks, tasks involving
1:12:571 Stunde, 12 Minuten, 57 Sekundencategorization, spatial reasoning, number, narrative sequencing, and compared their performance to
1:13:051 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 5 Sekundenhearing children of the same age, and to deaf children who had been exposed to a conventional sign language like American Sign Language.
1:13:151 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 15 SekundenAnd the results were nuanced in exactly the way you'd hope.
1:13:201 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 20 SekundenGiven everything we've been building toward on some tasks, the homesigners did just fine. Their basic categorization
1:13:291 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 29 Sekundenabilities were intact. They could group objects by shape or function. They understood cause and effect. They could track simple sequences of events.
1:13:411 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 41 SekundenThis tells you something important. It tells you that certain cognitive capacities are robust enough to develop
1:13:481 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 48 Sekundeneven without full linguistic input. The strong Warian idea that thought is impossible without language. These
1:13:571 Stunde, 13 Minuten, 57 Sekundenchildren are living proof that it's wrong. Thought exists without language.
1:14:031 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 3 SekundenCognition doesn't wait for words. But, and this is the crucial but, on other tasks, the homesigners struggled in
1:14:121 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 12 Sekundenspecific and revealing ways. tasks involving more complex numerical reasoning, for instance, or tasks that
1:14:211 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 21 Sekundenrequired them to think about other people's mental states, what psychologists call theory of mind, or
1:14:291 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 29 Sekundentasks that involved understanding certain kinds of spatial relationships that their home sign systems didn't
1:14:351 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 35 Sekundenencode. In these domains, the children without full language access showed genuine deficits compared to children
1:14:441 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 44 Sekundenwho had been exposed to a conventional language from birth. And the pattern of what they could and couldn't do mapped
1:14:531 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 53 Sekundenbeautifully onto the structure of their home sign systems.
1:14:571 Stunde, 14 Minuten, 57 SekundenWhere their gestural systems had developed ways of encoding a concept, their thinking about that concept was relatively intact.
1:15:071 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 7 Sekundenwhere their systems lacked the tools to encode something like precise number words or markers for someone else's beliefs versus reality.
1:15:171 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 17 SekundenTheir thinking in those domains was measurably weaker.
1:15:221 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 22 SekundenThis is powerful evidence. It's not just correlation. It's a natural experiment.
1:15:291 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 29 SekundenYou have children who differ primarily in their linguistic experience and you can see how that difference shapes their
1:15:361 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 36 Sekundencognitive abilities in domain specific ways. Language isn't creating thought from nothing but it's scaffolding
1:15:451 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 45 Sekundencertain kinds of thought. It's providing the cognitive infrastructure that makes certain complex operations possible or at least much easier.
1:15:561 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 56 SekundenGolden Meadow herself has been careful about how she frames this. She's pointed out that homesigners are not
1:16:041 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 4 Sekundenlanguageless in any absolute sense. They have a communication system. It's just impoverished compared to a full natural
1:16:131 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 13 Sekundenlanguage. So what you're really seeing is a dose response relationship.
1:16:201 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 20 SekundenMore linguistic structure leads to more cognitive capacity in the domains that structure supports. Less linguistic structure, less capacity.
1:16:301 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 30 SekundenIt's not all or nothing. It's graded.
1:16:351 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 35 SekundenAnd this connects to another body of research that was developing around the same time. Work on what happens when deaf individuals gain access to language
1:16:441 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 44 Sekundenlate. There are heartbreaking cases and I want to be respectful here because these are real people's lives of deaf
1:16:531 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 53 Sekundenindividuals who weren't diagnosed until they were adolescents or even adults who spent years or decades without any
1:17:001 Stunde, 17 Minutenlanguage at all. When researchers like Rachel Mayberry at UC San Diego studied these late learners, they found that the
1:17:091 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 9 Sekundenage at which you first encounter language has profound effects on your ultimate linguistic and cognitive abilities.
1:17:181 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 18 SekundenThe later the exposure, the harder it is to acquire full grammatical competence and the more pronounced certain
1:17:251 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 25 Sekundencognitive differences become. This dovetales with what we know about critical periods in brain development.
1:17:331 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 33 SekundenThe idea that there are windows of time during which the brain is especially primed to acquire certain kinds of
1:17:401 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 40 Sekundenskills and language is one of the most dramatic examples.
1:17:461 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 46 SekundenMiss that window and the architecture of cognition develops differently. not necessarily worse in every way, but
1:17:541 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 54 Sekundendifferently and in ways that reveal just how much of our typical cognitive toolkit depends on having had language during those formative years.
Kapitel 15: Numerical cognition
1:18:051 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 5 SekundenNow, there's a flip side to this story that's equally illuminating, and it comes from a place you might not expect.
1:18:131 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 13 SekundenNicaragua.
1:18:141 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 14 SekundenIn the late 1970s and early 1980s, after the Sandinista Revolution, the new
1:18:211 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 21 Sekundengovernment established the first schools for the deaf in Nicaraguan history.
1:18:271 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 27 SekundenBefore that, deaf Nicaraguans were largely isolated from each other, each developing their own home sign systems
1:18:351 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 35 Sekundenwithin their families, much like the children Golden Meadow studied. But when these children were brought together in
1:18:431 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 43 Sekundenschools, something extraordinary happened.
1:18:471 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 47 SekundenThe first cohort of students arrived with their individual home sign systems and began communicating with each other.
1:18:551 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 55 SekundenTheir teachers were trying to teach them to lipread Spanish, which mostly didn't work. But the children were doing something far more interesting on the
1:19:041 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 4 Sekundenplayground and in the hallways. They were pooling their home sign systems, negotiating shared meanings and
1:19:121 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 12 Sekundengradually developing a common communication system. This first generation system which linguists call
1:19:201 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 20 Sekundenlinguos nicaragu language with limited grammatical
1:19:281 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 28 Sekundenstructure. But here's where it gets truly wild.
1:19:331 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 33 SekundenWhen the next cohort of younger children arrived at the school a few years later, they were exposed to this pigeon from an
1:19:401 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 40 Sekundenearly age. And what they did with it was transform it. They didn't just learn it,
1:19:471 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 47 Sekundenthey complexified it. They added grammatical markers. They developed consistent rules for verb agreement, for
1:19:551 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 55 Sekundenspatial reference, for embedding one clause inside another. They turned a pigeon into a creole, a full rich
1:20:051 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 5 Sekundennatural language now known as idiom signos Nicaragu or Nicaraguan sign language. And they did this
1:20:131 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 13 Sekundenspontaneously as children without anyone teaching them to do it. Judy Keull and
1:20:201 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 20 SekundenSangos and other linguists who documented this process recognized it as something almost unprecedented.
1:20:281 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 28 Sekundenthe birth of a new language observed in real time under conditions that allowed scientific study.
1:20:351 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 35 SekundenSangas working with Golden Meadow and others was particularly interested in what the emergence of this language
1:20:421 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 42 Sekundenmeant for the cognitive abilities of its users.
1:20:461 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 46 SekundenAnd she found that the second generation signers, the ones who had access to the full grammatical language, outperformed
1:20:541 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 54 Sekundenthe first generation signers on certain cognitive tasks, specifically tasks involving spatial reasoning and theory
1:21:011 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 1 Sekundeof mind, same community, same schools, same cultural background. The primary
1:21:101 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 10 Sekundendifference was the richness of the linguistic system they'd been exposed to during childhood. And that difference in linguistic experience produced
1:21:181 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 18 Sekundenmeasurable differences in how they thought. The richer language didn't just let them communicate more effectively.
1:21:261 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 26 SekundenIt equipped them with cognitive tools that their predecessors with the more limited pigeon didn't fully have. Let
1:21:341 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 34 Sekundenthat settle for a moment. Because this Nicaraguan story is one of the most elegant natural experiments in the
1:21:431 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 43 Sekundenhistory of cognitive science, you can't ethically deprive children of language to study the effects.
1:21:511 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 51 SekundenBut history sometimes creates conditions that approximate that experiment. And when researchers are paying attention,
1:21:581 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 58 Sekundenthe results can be extraordinarily revealing.
1:22:021 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 2 SekundenAnd what they reveal is consistent with everything else we've been circling around. Language is not the source of
1:22:091 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 9 Sekundenthought. Thought exists without it. The home signers prove that. The first generation Nicaraguan signers prove
1:22:171 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 17 Sekundenthat. But language is a powerful amplifier and organizer of thought. It provides structures that make certain
1:22:251 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 25 Sekundencognitive operations easier, faster, more precise, and more sharable.
1:22:321 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 32 SekundenWithout those structures, you can still think, but there are ceilings you hit, particularly in domains that involve
1:22:411 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 41 Sekundenabstraction, precision, or reasoning about things that aren't physically present.
1:22:481 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 48 SekundenThis is actually a good moment to think about number because number is one of the domains where the evidence for
1:22:561 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 56 Sekundenlinguistic effects on cognition is strongest and most dramatic.
1:23:021 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 2 SekundenRemember how we talked about the pira earlier? Well, actually we haven't gotten to them yet in this telling and
1:23:101 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 10 Sekundenthey're too important to skip. So let me take you there now. The Pira are a small indigenous group living along the Mai
1:23:191 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 19 SekundenRiver in the Amazon rainforest of Brazil. There are only a few hundred of them and their language, also called
1:23:271 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 27 SekundenPira, has some features that have made it one of the most debated languages in modern linguistics.
1:23:341 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 34 SekundenThe person most associated with Pira research is Daniel Everett, a linguist who first went to the Amazon in the late
1:23:431 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 43 Sekunden1970s as a missionary and ended up spending decades living with the Pira, learning their language, and eventually
1:23:521 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 52 Sekundenpublishing claims that shook the foundations of linguistic theory.
1:23:571 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 57 SekundenAmong the many unusual features Everett documented, one stands out for our purposes.
1:24:041 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 4 SekundenPira has no precise number words, none.
1:24:081 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 8 SekundenThere are approximate terms that researchers have translated roughly as small amount and larger amount, but
1:24:161 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 16 Sekundennothing that consistently means 1, 2, 3, or any specific quantity. And this isn't
1:24:231 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 23 Sekundenbecause the pira can't perceive quantities.
1:24:261 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 26 SekundenIt's that their language doesn't encode them with precision.
1:24:311 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 31 SekundenSo what happens when you test pira speakers on numerical tasks?
1:24:361 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 36 SekundenPeter Gordon, a psychologist at Colia, published a study in 2004 in which he gave Pira speakers simple matching
1:24:451 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 45 Sekundentasks. Here are some nuts. Now put out the same number of nuts from your pile.
1:24:521 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 52 SekundenFor small quantities, one or two or three, they did fine. But as the quantities grew beyond three or four, their accuracy dropped dramatically.
1:25:031 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 3 SekundenThey could approximate, but they couldn't match exactly. And the errors weren't random. They showed the
1:25:111 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 11 Sekundensignature pattern of approximate number estimation.
1:25:141 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 14 SekundenThe same system that rats and infants use, a system that gets fuzzier as quantities get larger.
1:25:221 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 22 SekundenNow, this doesn't mean the pira are less intelligent. It means they're relying on the approximate number system that all
1:25:291 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 29 Sekundenhumans share without the precision boost that number words provide.
1:25:361 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 36 SekundenAnd studies with other groups support this interpretation.
1:25:401 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 40 SekundenElizabeth Spelka and others at Harvard have shown that precise number concepts, the ability to distinguish exactly seven
1:25:491 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 49 Sekundenfrom exactly 8, seem to depend on having words or symbols for those numbers.
1:25:551 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 55 SekundenWithout the words, you have the approximate sense, which is real and useful, but fundamentally imprecise.
1:26:041 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 4 SekundenHere's the thing that ties this back to our bigger story. Number words aren't just labels for quantities you already
1:26:111 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 11 Sekundenunderstand perfectly. They're cognitive tools that enable a kind of thinking you literally cannot do without them. They
1:26:191 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 19 Sekundencreate discrete categories out of a continuous approximate sense. They let you hold a precise quantity in working
1:26:261 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 26 Sekundenmemory, manipulate it, combine it with other quantities.
1:26:321 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 32 SekundenThe language doesn't just describe numerical thought. It makes certain numerical thought possible. And if
1:26:391 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 39 Sekundenthat's true for number, and the evidence is strong that it is, then it's probably true to varying degrees for other cognitive domains as well.
1:26:501 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 50 SekundenSpatial reasoning, temporal reasoning, social cognition, emotional granularity.
1:26:581 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 58 SekundenIn each of these domains, we've seen evidence that the linguistic tools available to you shape the cognitive operations you can perform. Not
1:27:071 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 7 Sekundenabsolutely, not as a prison, but as infrastructure, as scaffolding, as the difference
1:27:141 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 14 Sekundenbetween having a calculator and doing math in your head with your fingers.
