1:15:471 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 47 Sekundenof resisting or not resisting, every participant was given a set of geometric puzzles to work on. The puzzles were
1:15:561 Stunde, 15 Minuten, 56 Sekundenunbeknownst to the participants unsolvable.
1:16:011 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 1 SekundeWhat the researchers actually measured was how long people would persist before
1:16:071 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 7 Sekunden[clears throat]
1:16:081 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 8 Sekundengiving up.
1:16:101 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 10 SekundenAnd here's what they found. The people who had been allowed to eat the cookies, who hadn't had to resist anything,
1:16:181 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 18 Sekundenworked on the puzzles for an average of about 20 minutes. The people who had spent the previous period resisting the
1:16:261 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 26 Sekundencookies, forcing their attention away from the chocolate, exercising self-control, lasted about 8 minutes,
1:16:341 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 34 Sekundenless than half the time. Bowmeister's interpretation was striking. He proposed
1:16:401 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 40 Sekundenthat self-control and by extension the kind of effortful attention the executive network provides draws on a
1:16:491 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 49 Sekundensingle limited resource like a muscle that fatigues with use.
1:16:551 Stunde, 16 Minuten, 55 SekundenYou spend some of that resource resisting cookies and you have less of it available for persisting at a
1:17:021 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 2 Sekundendifficult puzzle. He called the resource ego in the old Freudian sense of the executive self and the state of having used it up he called ego depletion.
1:17:141 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 14 SekundenThe paper published in 1998 landed like a stone in a pond. The ripples went everywhere.
1:17:231 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 23 SekundenWithin a few years, ego depletion had become one of the most cited ideas in social psychology.
1:17:301 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 30 SekundenHundreds of studies seemed to confirm it.
1:17:341 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 34 SekundenResearchers found depletion effects in everything from attention tasks to moral decision-making to consumer choices. The
1:17:411 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 41 Sekundenstory was elegant and it felt deeply true. You've had those days, the ones where you've been making decisions and
1:17:491 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 49 Sekundenmanaging your focus and dealing with difficult people since 7 in the morning.
1:17:541 Stunde, 17 Minuten, 54 SekundenAnd by evening, you find yourself staring at a cupboard, eating cereal out of the box because you simply cannot summon the will to decide what to cook.
1:18:061 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 6 SekundenEgo depletion said that wasn't a character flaw. It was a resource problem. You'd used up the stuff that
1:18:131 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 13 Sekundenself-control runs on. Bowmeister and his collaborators even proposed a specific mechanism. They suggested the resource
1:18:221 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 22 Sekundenmight be glucose, actual blood sugar that the brain burns through when it exercises control.
1:18:291 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 29 SekundenThere were studies showing that drinking a glass of lemonade sweetened with real sugar seemed to restore depleted self-control,
1:18:381 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 38 Sekundenwhile lemonade with artificial sweetener did not. It was a tidy, almost mechanical story. Willpower is fuel.
1:18:471 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 47 Sekundenattention as a tank that empties. And then the whole thing started to wobble.
1:18:531 Stunde, 18 Minuten, 53 SekundenThe first serious tremor came in 2010 when a team led by the psychologist Evan Carter began running meta analyses,
1:19:021 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 2 Sekundenwhich are studies that pull the data from many individual studies to see whether the overall effect is real and how large it actually is.
1:19:121 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 12 SekundenWhat Carter's group found was troubling.
1:19:151 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 15 SekundenWhen you accounted for publication bias, the tendency for journals to publish positive results and file away null
1:19:221 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 22 Sekundenones, the ego depletion effect shrank dramatically. In some analyses, it nearly disappeared.
1:19:311 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 31 SekundenThis didn't prove the effect wasn't real, but it suggested that the published literature was giving an
1:19:371 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 37 Sekundeninflated picture. Then in 2016 came the really big test. A massive
1:19:441 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 44 Sekundenpre-registered replication effort involving over 2,000 participants across two dozen laboratories around the world
1:19:531 Stunde, 19 Minuten, 53 Sekundentried to reproduce the ego depletion effect using a standardized protocol.
1:20:001 Stunde, 20 MinutenPre-registered means the researchers committed to their methods and analyses in advance so they couldn't quietly
1:20:071 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 7 Sekundenadjust things after seeing the data and the result was essentially null. The average depletion effect across all
1:20:161 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 16 Sekundenthose labs was so small it was statistically indistinguishable from zero.
1:20:241 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 24 SekundenNow, this is where the story gets genuinely interesting rather than just deflating because Balmeister and his supporters didn't simply fold.
1:20:331 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 33 SekundenThey argued with some justification that the specific task used in the replication might not have been the
1:20:411 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 41 Sekundenright one. That ego depletion is real but harder to produce under certain conditions. that the original phenomenon
1:20:501 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 50 Sekundenis more nuanced than a simple onoff switch.
1:20:551 Stunde, 20 Minuten, 55 SekundenAnd there are still researchers who find depletion effects in their labs, particularly when the depleting task is
1:21:021 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 2 Sekundenmore personally meaningful or emotionally demanding. The debate is ongoing, and it's a real debate, not a
1:21:101 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 10 Sekundensettled case. But the glucose theory, the idea that willpower literally runs on blood sugar, has fared much worse.
1:21:201 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 20 SekundenThe brain does use glucose, of course.
1:21:231 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 23 SekundenIt's the organ's primary fuel, but the amount of extra glucose consumed during a difficult cognitive task is tiny, a
1:21:321 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 32 Sekundenfraction of a calorie, and the brain has robust mechanisms for maintaining its own fuel supply.
1:21:401 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 40 SekundenThe lemonade studies, it turned out, had small samples and some methodological issues.
1:21:481 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 48 SekundenLater work suggested that just swishing a sweet liquid in your mouth without swallowing it could produce similar
1:21:551 Stunde, 21 Minuten, 55 Sekundeneffects, which points toward motivation and reward signaling rather than actual fuel depletion.
1:22:041 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 4 SekundenYour brain is not running out of gas when you feel mentally exhausted.
1:22:081 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 8 Sekundensomething else is happening. And this is where the story connects back to attention in a way that's genuinely useful. Because even if the strong
1:22:181 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 18 Sekundenversion of ego depletion, the idea of a single depletable resource, doesn't hold up cleanly, the subjective experience it
1:22:261 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 26 Sekundenwas trying to explain is absolutely real. You do get worse at controlling your attention over the course of a long
1:22:341 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 34 Sekundendemanding day. You do find it harder to resist distractions at 4 in the afternoon than at 9 in the morning. The
1:22:431 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 43 Sekundenquestion is just what's actually causing that?
1:22:471 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 47 SekundenOne alternative comes from a psychologist named Michael Inselct at the University of Toronto who has
1:22:541 Stunde, 22 Minuten, 54 Sekundenproposed what he calls a motivational shift model. The idea is that what feels like depletion is actually a change in motivation.
1:23:041 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 4 SekundenAfter a long period of effortful control, your brain doesn't run out of some resource. Instead, it shifts its priorities.
1:23:131 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 13 SekundenIt becomes less interested in have to tasks, the ones that require top- down executive control and more interested in
1:23:221 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 22 Sekundenwant to tasks, the ones that feel rewarding or pleasurable.
1:23:281 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 28 SekundenThink about what that means for your executive network. It's not that the network can't fire anymore. It's that
1:23:351 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 35 Sekundenthe brain's motivational systems are gently pulling you away from using it.
1:23:411 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 41 SekundenYou could still focus if something truly urgent demanded it. A fire alarm at 4:30 in the afternoon would snap your
1:23:491 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 49 Sekundenattention into place instantly. But in the absence of genuine urgency, the system tilts toward rest, toward
1:23:571 Stunde, 23 Minuten, 57 Sekundennovelty, toward reward. And that looks and feels exactly like running out of willpower. Even though the underlying
1:24:051 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 5 Sekundenmechanism is completely different, there's another framework worth sitting with from the psychologist Robert Hawk
Kapitel 16: The rhythm of attention
1:24:131 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 13 Sekundenwho proposed something called compensatory control theory. Hockeyy's idea is that maintaining focused attention in the face of fatigue or
1:24:221 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 22 Sekundendifficulty doesn't just happen automatically.
1:24:261 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 26 SekundenThe brain has to actively compensate, recruiting additional resources, increasing effort, working harder to maintain the same level of performance.
1:24:371 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 37 SekundenAnd this compensatory effort has costs.
1:24:401 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 40 SekundenIt produces subjective fatigue, increased stress, and a growing reluctance to continue.
1:24:481 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 48 SekundenPerformance might hold steady for a while, but only because the system is working harder and harder behind the scenes to keep it there. Eventually,
1:24:561 Stunde, 24 Minuten, 56 Sekundenthe cost becomes too high and performance drops. Not because a resource ran out, but because the brain essentially decides the costbenefit ratio no longer justifies the effort.
1:25:081 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 8 SekundenYou've felt this. Think about reading a dense paragraph for the fourth time because your mind keeps sliding off it.
1:25:171 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 17 SekundenYou can force yourself back. The executive network can reassert control, but each time it does, the effort feels
1:25:261 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 26 Sekundenheavier and the pull towards something easier, checking your phone, getting a snack, just closing your eyes for a second, grows stronger.
1:25:361 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 36 SekundenThat's not a broken system. That's a system doing exactly what it should, protecting itself from unsustainable
1:25:431 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 43 Sekundenexpenditure by making continued effort feel progressively less appealing.
1:25:491 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 49 SekundenAnd this connects to something fascinating about how the brain handles fatigue more broadly.
1:25:551 Stunde, 25 Minuten, 55 SekundenThere's growing evidence that the feeling of mental exhaustion is not a direct readout of some depleted substance.
1:26:031 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 3 SekundenIt's more like a signal, a forecast generated by the brain to regulate your behavior. In the same way that thirst
1:26:121 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 12 Sekundendoesn't mean your cells are literally drying out right now, but rather that the brain is predicting a future need
1:26:191 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 19 Sekundenand motivating you to act. Mental fatigue may be the brain's way of saying, "We've been running the expensive systems for a long time. And
1:26:281 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 28 Sekundencontinuing at this rate carries risks, so let's feel tired and shift to something less costly."
1:26:351 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 35 SekundenThe fatigue is real. The experience is genuine. But it's a regulatory signal, not a fuel gauge.
1:26:441 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 44 SekundenA researcher named Andre Lorist working at the University of Grooningan has done some interesting work using brain
1:26:521 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 52 Sekundenimaging to look at what happens during sustained attention tasks as people get more fatigued.
1:26:581 Stunde, 26 Minuten, 58 SekundenWhat she and her colleagues have found is that the brain doesn't simply go quiet. Instead, the patterns of activation change.
1:27:081 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 8 SekundenAreas associated with executive control show altered firing and areas associated with reward processing and default mode
1:27:171 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 17 Sekundenactivity begin to intrude more. The brain is not shutting down. It's reorganizing its priorities.
1:27:271 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 27 SekundenThe salience network, that toggle switch we talked about earlier, seems to shift its bias.
1:27:341 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 34 SekundenEarly in a task, it favors routing control to the executive network. As fatigue builds, it increasingly lets the
1:27:431 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 43 Sekundendefault mode network creep back in. Mind wandering increases.
1:27:491 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 49 SekundenTask irrelevant thoughts surface more often, not because you've lost the ability to focus, but because the brain's internal
1:27:571 Stunde, 27 Minuten, 57 Sekundenarbitration is gradually changing its vote about what matters most right now.
1:28:041 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 4 SekundenWhich means that the experience you have at the end of a long day, that scattered, foggy, easily distracted
1:28:111 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 11 Sekundenfeeling, is not a sign that your attention is damaged or that modern life has broken something in you.
1:28:201 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 20 SekundenIt's the feeling of a system that has been working hard all day, doing exactly what these networks evolved to do, and is now shifting toward recovery.
1:28:311 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 31 SekundenThe executive network is not empty. The salience network is not broken. The default mode network is not invading.
1:28:401 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 40 SekundenThe whole system is just rebalancing.
1:28:431 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 43 SekundenThe way your legs feel heavy after a long walk, not because they're injured, but because they've been doing their
1:28:501 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 50 Sekundenjob. And this rebalancing happens on shorter time scales, too. Not just across a whole day. Even within a single
1:28:591 Stunde, 28 Minuten, 59 Sekundenhour of focused work, your attention naturally oscillates.
1:29:041 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 4 SekundenThere are microlapses, moments where the default mode network briefly surfaces and then recedes. And
1:29:111 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 11 Sekundenthese seem to be a normal and possibly necessary part of sustained attention rather than a failure of it.
1:29:191 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 19 SekundenA researcher named Paul Aley has described attention as inherently rhythmic, pulsing between engagement and
1:29:271 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 27 Sekundendisengagement on cycles that last seconds to minutes. The brain cannot sustain a single unwavering beam of
1:29:361 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 36 Sekundenfocus any more than your heart can sustain a single unending contraction. The rhythm is the function.
1:29:451 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 45 SekundenSo when you lie here and notice your attention drifting, wandering away from these words and then coming back, that
1:29:541 Stunde, 29 Minuten, 54 Sekundenoscillation is not you failing to pay attention. It's attention working exactly as it's designed to work in
1:30:021 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 2 Sekundenwaves, in pulses, in a rhythm that your brain has been running since long before you were born.
1:30:101 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 10 SekundenAnd there's something quietly reassuring about that, about knowing that the drift is built
1:30:161 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 16 Sekundenin, that the system expects it, that it's part of the design rather than a flaw in it. But there's still a piece of
Kapitel 17: Attention and consciousness
1:30:251 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 25 Sekundenthis story we haven't touched, and it might be the most important one for understanding what attention actually is
1:30:331 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 33 Sekundenin the deepest sense. Because everything we've talked about so far, the networks,
1:30:391 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 39 Sekundenthe chemicals, the switching costs, the fatigue signals, all of it describes the machinery.
1:30:481 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 48 SekundenWhat it doesn't quite explain is something more fundamental, something that philosophers and neuroscientists have been circling for
1:30:561 Stunde, 30 Minuten, 56 Sekundendecades now, and that is the relationship between attention and consciousness.
1:31:021 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 2 SekundenBecause once you start pulling on that thread, the whole picture shifts.
1:31:071 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 7 SekundenThink about what it means to be aware of something.
1:31:111 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 11 SekundenRight now, as you listen to these words, there is a voice arriving in your ears.
1:31:171 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 17 SekundenAnd somehow that voice is not just vibrating your eardrums. It is being heard. There is something it is like to
1:31:251 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 25 Sekundenhear it. There is an experience happening, a felt quality, a presence of sound in your mind that is more than
1:31:341 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 34 Sekundenjust information being processed. And the question that has haunted both philosophy and neuroscience for a very
1:31:411 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 41 Sekundenlong time is whether attention is the thing that makes that experience possible.
1:31:481 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 48 SekundenWhether attention is the gate through which raw neural activity becomes conscious experience or whether
1:31:551 Stunde, 31 Minuten, 55 Sekundenconsciousness and attention are two separate things that happen to overlap most of the time but can under the right
1:32:031 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 3 Sekundenconditions come apart. It sounds abstract but it's not really. It's about something you do every single night.
1:32:141 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 14 SekundenRight now, as you lie here, you are probably aware of the weight of your body against the surface beneath you.
1:32:221 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 22 SekundenBut a moment ago, before I mentioned it, were you? The pressure was there. The
1:32:301 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 30 Sekundennerve endings were firing. The signal was arriving in your somato sensory cortex, but without attention directed toward
1:32:381 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 38 Sekundenit. Was there an experience of that pressure? Was there something it was like to feel it? Or was it just data sitting in a buffer unread?
1:32:521 Stunde, 32 Minuten, 52 SekundenThis is the question that a philosopher named Ned Block has spent decades trying to sharpen. Block who works at New York
1:33:001 Stunde, 33 MinutenUniversity drew a distinction in the mid 1990s that has become one of the most debated ideas in the science of consciousness.
1:33:091 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 9 SekundenHe proposed that there are two different kinds of awareness and that most of the confusion in the field comes from
1:33:171 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 17 Sekundenmashing them together. The first kind he called access consciousness.
1:33:221 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 22 SekundenThis is the kind of awareness where information is available to your reasoning, your decision making, your verbal report.
1:33:321 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 32 SekundenIf someone asks you what you're thinking about and you can answer, that content is access conscious. It's the
1:33:401 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 40 Sekundeninformation that has made it into the workspace where your executive network can use it, manipulate it, act on it.
1:33:481 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 48 SekundenThe second kind he called phenomenal consciousness.
1:33:521 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 52 SekundenThis is harder to pin down and that's sort of the point. Phenomenal consciousness is the raw feel of
1:34:001 Stunde, 34 Minutenexperience, the redness of red, the ache of a headache, the warmth of sunlight on
1:34:071 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 7 Sekundenyour arm. It's what philosophers sometimes call qualia, though that word has its own baggage.
1:34:151 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 15 SekundenBlock's claim, and this is the part that stirred everything up, was that phenomenal consciousness can exist without access consciousness.
1:34:241 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 24 Sekundenthat you can have an experience, a genuine felt qualitative experience without that experience being available
1:34:321 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 32 Sekundento your reasoning mind, without you being able to report it or even know it's happening.
1:34:391 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 39 SekundenAnd here's what's strange about this. It sounds paradoxical.
1:34:441 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 44 SekundenHow can you experience something without knowing you're experiencing it? But Block pointed to cases that seem to show
1:34:511 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 51 Sekundenexactly that. One of the most compelling involves a phenomenon called inattentional blindness, which we
1:34:591 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 59 Sekundentouched on earlier when we talked about how attention selects. In the famous gorilla experiment, the one Daniel
1:35:061 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 6 SekundenSimons and Christopher Shabri ran in 1999, people watching a basketball passing
1:35:141 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 14 Sekundenvideo completely failed to notice a person in a gorilla suit walking through the scene. They didn't see it. They
1:35:211 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 21 Sekundenwould swear under oath they didn't see it. But Block asks a genuinely interesting question about what didn't
1:35:291 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 29 Sekundensee it means the light from the gorilla hit their retinas. Their visual cortex
1:35:361 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 36 Sekundenprocessed it. There is some evidence from related studies using eye tracking that people's eyes sometimes fixate
1:35:441 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 44 Sekundendirectly on the unexpected object and still fail to report it.
1:35:491 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 49 SekundenSo was the gorilla phenomenally conscious, experienced but not accessed or was it truly absent from experience
1:35:571 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 57 Sekundenaltogether, just data that never became anything felt?
1:36:031 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 3 SekundenBlock says yes, there was probably a phenomenal experience there that never made it into access.
1:36:101 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 10 SekundenMany other researchers say, "No, that's an illusion." That if you weren't aware of it in any reportable sense, then
1:36:181 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 18 Sekundenthere was no experience to speak of. And the reason this disagreement matters so much for our story is that it's really a disagreement about what attention does.
1:36:281 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 28 SekundenIf block is right, then attention is not the thing that creates conscious experience. Attention
1:36:361 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 36 Sekundenis the thing that selects which conscious experiences get promoted into the workspace where you can think about them, talk about them, remember them.
1:36:481 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 48 SekundenConsciousness is broader than attention.
1:36:511 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 51 SekundenIt's happening all over the place and attention is just the spotlight that picks out which parts of it you get to know about.
1:37:001 Stunde, 37 MinutenBut if block is wrong, if the opposing view is correct, then attention might be much more fundamental than a selection
1:37:081 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 8 Sekundenmechanism. It might be the very thing that turns neural processing into experience.
1:37:151 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 15 SekundenNo attention, no experience.
1:37:181 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 18 SekundenNot no access to the experience, but no experience at all.
1:37:241 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 24 SekundenThe person who has pushed this second view most forcefully is a psychologist named Jesse Prince who argues that
1:37:321 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 32 Sekundenattention is both necessary and sufficient for conscious experience.
1:37:381 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 38 SekundenPrince's position, which he laid out in detail in a book published in 2012, is that when you attend to something,
1:37:471 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 47 Sekundenthat attending is what makes it conscious. And when you withdraw attention, the processing continues, but the experience stops.
1:37:571 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 57 SekundenThe gorilla wasn't experienced and then forgotten. It was never experienced in the first place. The visual system
1:38:041 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 4 Sekundenprocessed it the way a security camera processes footage that nobody is watching. The information was there, but
1:38:121 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 12 Sekundennobody was home to see it. Now, you might be thinking that this sounds like the kind of argument that could go back
1:38:191 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 19 Sekundenand forth forever without anyone winning. And you'd be partly right. It is genuinely hard to resolve because the
1:38:291 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 29 Sekundenthing you'd need to measure, the presence or absence of subjective experience independent of someone's ability to report it, is exactly the
1:38:381 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 38 Sekundenthing that's hardest to measure in all of science.
1:38:421 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 42 SekundenIf someone can't report an experience, how do you know they had one? And if they can report it, then by definition,
1:38:501 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 50 Sekundenit was accessed. So you can't use it to demonstrate phenomenal consciousness without access.
1:38:581 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 58 SekundenBlock is aware of this problem. Everyone is. It's part of what makes consciousness research so maddening and
1:39:071 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 7 Sekundenso fascinating at the same time. But there are some experimental findings that push the conversation forward in interesting ways.
Kapitel 18: The boundaries of experience
1:39:171 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 17 SekundenOne line of evidence comes from studies of something called the attentional blink. This is a phenomenon discovered
1:39:241 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 24 Sekundenin the early 1990s by Jane Raymond, Kiman Shapiro, and Karen Arnell. Here's
1:39:321 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 32 Sekundenwhat happens. If you show someone a rapid stream of items, letters, or numbers flashing on a screen, one after
1:39:391 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 39 Sekundenanother, and you ask them to watch for two targets, say a white letter and then a black letter, something peculiar
1:39:471 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 47 Sekundenoccurs. If the second target appears within about 200 to 500 milliseconds after the first one, people often miss
1:39:561 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 56 Sekundenit completely. It's as if attending to the first target temporarily blinds them to the second one. The attentional
1:40:051 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 5 Sekundensystem blinks like an eye. And the interesting thing is that when you look at what the brain is doing during this
1:40:121 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 12 Sekundenblink using brain imaging, the missed item still gets processed to a surprisingly high level. The visual
1:40:211 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 21 Sekundencortex responds to it. Even some meaning related areas respond to it. The word
1:40:271 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 27 Sekundengets read in some sense by the brain, but it never makes it into the person's awareness.
1:40:351 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 35 SekundenSo what happened there? Was there a flicker of experience that got snuffed out before it could be reported? Or was
1:40:431 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 43 Sekundenthere processing without any experience at all? The data doesn't cleanly answer that. And this is one of those places
1:40:501 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 50 Sekundenwhere honesty requires saying that the science is genuinely stuck. Not because it's bad science, but because the
1:40:571 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 57 Sekundenquestion is that hard. What the attentional blink does tell us though is something important about the temporal structure of attention.
1:41:071 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 7 SekundenIt takes time for the system to process one thing consciously.
1:41:121 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 12 SekundenAnd during that time, the gate is at least partly closed. Attention is not instantaneous.
1:41:191 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 19 SekundenIt has a rhythm, a recovery period, a refractory window. And this connects back to something we talked about
1:41:281 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 28 Sekundenearlier, the idea from that attention is inherently oscilly, pulsing rather than streaming.
1:41:361 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 36 SekundenThe attentional blink might be one expression of that deeper rhythm, the systems need to close briefly in order to open fully.
1:41:451 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 45 SekundenThere's another angle on this attention consciousness question that comes from a very different tradition and it's worth sitting with for a moment because it
1:41:541 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 54 Sekundenbrings the whole conversation back to something you can feel. Contemplative traditions, particularly certain forms
1:42:021 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 2 Sekundenof Buddhist meditation, have long made a distinction between focused attention and what they call open awareness, a
1:42:101 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 10 Sekundenstate in which you are conscious, vividly so, but not attending to anything in particular. Experienced
1:42:181 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 18 Sekundenmeditators describe this as a kind of wide openen wakefulness, a panoramic consciousness without a center, without
1:42:251 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 25 Sekundena selected object. And when neuroscientists began studying experienced meditators in the early 2000s, particularly in the work coming
1:42:351 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 35 Sekundenout of Richard Davidson's lab at the University of Wisconsin, they found something intriguing.
