1:33:041 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 4 SekundenIt was organized, coherent, almost musical in its synchronization.
1:33:101 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 10 SekundenAnd here's what matters for our story about being alone.
1:33:141 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 14 SekundenWhen Ricard and other long-term practitioners were scanned at rest, just sitting quietly with no specific
1:33:211 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 21 Sekundenmeditation instruction, their default mode networks looked different, too. Not
1:33:281 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 28 Sekundenshut down, not suppressed, but quieter in the regions associated with self-referential rumination and more
1:33:361 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 36 Sekundenactive in regions associated with present moment awareness and intraception. the sense of the body from the inside.
1:33:451 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 45 SekundenAntoine Lutz working with Davidson published some of the key papers on this in the mid 2000s and the findings have
1:33:531 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 53 Sekundenbeen broadly supported since though with the usual caveats.
1:33:581 Stunde, 33 Minuten, 58 SekundenThese are small samples. The practitioners are unusual people. You can't easily separate the effects of
1:34:061 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 6 Sekundenmeditation from the effects of being the kind of person who devotes their life to meditation.
1:34:141 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 14 SekundenBut the pattern has held up across multiple labs and multiple contemplative traditions.
1:34:211 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 21 SekundenSomething about sustained practice in sitting with your own mind changes the way the default mode network behaves
1:34:301 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 30 Sekundenwhen you're alone. And this connects back to something we touched on earlier, Judson Brewer's work at Yale.
1:34:381 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 38 SekundenBrewer, the psychiatrist who studied meditators and found that experienced practitioners showed reduced activity in
1:34:461 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 46 Sekundenthe posterior singulate cortex, one of the major hubs of the default mode network.
1:34:541 Stunde, 34 Minuten, 54 SekundenThat's the region most associated with mind wandering and self-reerential thought. The MI center, the part that's always narrating your life back to you.
1:35:061 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 6 SekundenIn experienced meditators, it was still active, but it was more tightly coupled with regions involved in cognitive control.
1:35:161 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 16 SekundenThe interpretation, and Brewer has been careful about this, is not that meditation silences the default mode
1:35:231 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 23 Sekundennetwork. It's that it changes the relationship between the network and the rest of the brain. The inner monologue
1:35:311 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 31 Sekundenstill runs, but it runs with less stickiness, less of that quality where a thought grabs you and pulls you down a
1:35:401 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 40 Sekundencorridor of worry before you even notice you've left the room.
1:35:451 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 45 SekundenThink about what that means for the experience of being alone.
1:35:491 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 49 SekundenIf the default mode network is the engine of solitude, the thing that fills the silence when external input drops
1:35:571 Stunde, 35 Minuten, 57 Sekundenaway, then the quality of your solitude depends enormously on how that engine runs. For someone whose default mode
1:36:061 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 6 Sekundentends toward rumination, toward replaying social failures, rehearsing future catastrophes,
1:36:131 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 13 Sekundenpicking at old wounds, solitude feels like being locked in a room with an unfriendly narrator. For someone whose
1:36:221 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 22 Sekundendefault mode has been trained, or perhaps was temperamentally inclined to observe its own contents without getting
1:36:301 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 30 Sekundentangled in them, solitude feels like something else entirely.
1:36:351 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 35 SekundenNot empty, not quiet exactly, but spacious.
1:36:411 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 41 SekundenThe thoughts still come. They just don't stick as hard. And this is where the contemplative insight and the
1:36:491 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 49 Sekundenneuroscience converge on something genuinely useful.
1:36:531 Stunde, 36 Minuten, 53 SekundenBecause the skill that meditation traditions have been teaching for millennia, the ability to notice a thought without becoming it, to let the
1:37:021 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 2 Sekundeninner monologue run without believing every word of it, turns out to map surprisingly well onto what the brain
1:37:101 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 10 Sekundenimaging is showing. The default mode network isn't the enemy. Rumination isn't the default mode network's only
1:37:201 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 20 Sekundensetting. The network can run in a mode that's integrative, creative, restorative.
1:37:281 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 28 SekundenAll the things we talked about when we discussed the positive architecture of solitude. The question is whether you can let it do that or whether every
1:37:371 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 37 Sekundenmoment of quiet triggers the threat scanning socially anxious pattern that Kaciopo's work described so well.
Kapitel 18: Mindfulness and cognition
1:37:471 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 47 SekundenWhich brings us to something practical and something that the clinical literature has started to take seriously.
1:37:541 Stunde, 37 Minuten, 54 SekundenMindfulness-based interventions which are essentially secularized stripped down versions of contemplative attention
1:38:021 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 2 Sekundentraining have shown consistent effects on exactly this dimension.
1:38:071 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 7 SekundenZindel Seagel working in Toronto in the late 1990s developed mindfulness-based
1:38:141 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 14 Sekundencognitive therapy or MBCT originally as a relapse prevention tool for depression.
1:38:221 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 22 SekundenThe core insight was simple but powerful.
1:38:261 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 26 SekundenPeople who've been depressed aren't just sad. They've developed a cognitive habit
1:38:331 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 33 Sekundenwhere a small dip in mood triggers a cascade of ruminative self-referential thought. Why do I always feel this way?
1:38:421 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 42 SekundenWhat's wrong with me? This will never change. And that cascade deepens the dip into a full episode. MBCT teaches people
1:38:511 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 51 Sekundento notice the cascade starting and to step back from it, to see the thoughts as thoughts rather than as facts about
1:38:591 Stunde, 38 Minuten, 59 Sekundenthemselves. And it works. Multiple randomized controlled trials have shown it reduces relapse rates in people with
1:39:071 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 7 Sekundenrecurrent depression by roughly 40 to 50% compared to usual care, which is a
1:39:151 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 15 Sekundensubstantial effect for a psychological intervention.
1:39:191 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 19 SekundenBut here's what's interesting for our purposes. The mechanism isn't about being with other people. It isn't social
1:39:271 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 27 Sekundensupport. It isn't distraction. It isn't restructuring the content of the thoughts. It's about changing the
1:39:351 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 35 Sekundenperson's relationship with their own inner experience. It's teaching them in a very concrete and practiced way to be
1:39:441 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 44 Sekundenmore comfortable alone with their own mind. To sit with a difficult thought without it becoming a crisis, to let the
1:39:521 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 52 Sekundendefault mode network do its thing without every activation becoming a spiral.
1:39:581 Stunde, 39 Minuten, 58 SekundenAnd you've almost certainly felt the difference between these two modes yourself.
1:40:041 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 4 SekundenEven without any formal training, there are evenings when you're alone and the silence feels like a gift. When your
1:40:131 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 13 Sekundenmind wanders in pleasant associative ways, when you find yourself remembering something funny, or imagining something
1:40:211 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 21 Sekundenyou'd like to build, or noticing the texture of the light in the room. And there are evenings when the same silence feels oppressive.
1:40:311 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 31 SekundenWhen every thought seems to lead back to something you're worried about.
1:40:361 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 36 SekundenWhen you reach for your phone, not because you want to see anything in particular, but because the alternative,
1:40:431 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 43 Sekundenjust sitting there with whatever your mind is serving up, feels intolerable.
1:40:491 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 49 SekundenSame person, same room, same default mode network, different weather inside.
1:40:551 Stunde, 40 Minuten, 55 SekundenAs we said, and the difference isn't random. It tracks with how tired you are, how socially nourished or depleted
1:41:031 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 3 Sekundenyou feel, how much unresolved stress is sitting in the queue.
1:41:081 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 8 SekundenBut it also tracks, and this is the part that matters, with practice, with how much experience you have noticing what
1:41:161 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 16 Sekundenyour mind is doing and choosing gently not to follow every thread it offers.
1:41:231 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 23 SekundenSo the picture that's emerging from the neuroscience, from the clinical work, from the contemplative traditions is
1:41:311 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 31 Sekundenthat being comfortable alone isn't a personality trait you either have or you don't. It's closer to a skill or maybe a
1:41:401 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 40 Sekundencapacity that exists on a spectrum and can be developed.
1:41:451 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 45 SekundenWinnott saw it as a developmental achievement rooted in early attachment.
1:41:511 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 51 SekundenThe attachment researchers confirmed that secure attachment makes it easier.
1:41:561 Stunde, 41 Minuten, 56 SekundenBut easier isn't only the clinical evidence suggests that even people who didn't start with that secure
1:42:041 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 4 Sekundenbase can learn to relate differently to their own inner experience.
1:42:091 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 9 SekundenIt takes work. It takes practice. It often takes help. But the capacity isn't fixed at birth or sealed in childhood.
1:42:191 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 19 SekundenAnd there's something quietly hopeful about that because it means the paradox we named earlier, the one where the people who
1:42:271 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 27 Sekundenmost need solitude are the ones who find it hardest, isn't a trap. It's a starting point. The discomfort isn't a
1:42:361 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 36 Sekundensign that you're broken or that solitude isn't for you. It's a sign that the skill hasn't been practiced yet, that
1:42:441 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 44 Sekundenthe default mode network is running in its threat scanning mode because that's the mode it knows best, and that a
1:42:521 Stunde, 42 Minuten, 52 Sekundendifferent mode is available. One where being alone with yourself feels less like confinement and more like company.
1:43:011 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 1 SekundeThe kind of company that doesn't ask anything of you, doesn't need you to perform or respond,
1:43:081 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 8 Sekundenjust the quiet company of a mind that has been yours all along and is when you let it settle surprisingly okay to sit
1:43:171 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 17 Sekundenwith. And that word company is worth pausing on because one of the stranger things about the psychology of being
1:43:251 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 25 Sekundenalone is that the language we reach for almost always frames it as an absence.
1:43:311 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 31 Sekundenalone, isolated, solitary, without. The words describe
1:43:371 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 37 Sekundenwhat's missing. the other people, the conversation, the warmth of a shared room, but the experience of comfortable
1:43:461 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 46 Sekundensolitude, the kind that Winnott described, the kind that the contemplative traditions have been cultivating for thousands of years, the
1:43:551 Stunde, 43 Minuten, 55 Sekundenkind that shows up in the brain as a calmer, less sticky default mode network. That experience isn't really
1:44:021 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 2 Sekundenabout absence at all. It's about presence.
1:44:071 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 7 SekundenBeing present to your own thinking, your own feeling, your own quiet sense of existing in a body, in a room on a
1:44:161 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 16 Sekundenparticular night. The philosopher William James back in the 1890s made a distinction that still holds up. He
1:44:241 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 24 Sekundentalked about the difference between the self as knower and the self as known.
1:44:301 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 30 Sekundenthe I that does the experiencing and the me that gets experienced.
1:44:361 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 36 SekundenWhen you're comfortable alone, both of those are in the room. You're not just a mind rattling around in silence. You're
1:44:441 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 44 Sekundena mind aware of itself and finding that awareness, if not always pleasant, at least bearable, at least interesting.
1:44:541 Stunde, 44 Minuten, 54 SekundenAnd this is where the science and the contemplative traditions land in roughly the same place, which doesn't happen as
1:45:011 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 1 Sekundeoften as you'd think. The Buddhist concept of mindfulness, stripped of its spiritual framing and studied in the
1:45:091 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 9 Sekundenlab, turns out to describe something quite precise.
1:45:141 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 14 SekundenIt's the capacity to observe your own mental states without being consumed by them. To notice that you're anxious without becoming the anxiety.
1:45:251 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 25 SekundenTo notice that the silence feels heavy without deciding that the heaviness means something is wrong. Zindel
1:45:321 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 32 SekundenSeagel's work with mindfulness-based cognitive therapy which we touched on showed that this capacity can be trained
1:45:401 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 40 Sekundenand the training doesn't require a monastery or a decade of practice. It requires at its most basic level
1:45:481 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 48 Sekundenrepeated gentle exposure to your own inner weather which is when you think about it exactly what solitude provides.
1:45:571 Stunde, 45 Minuten, 57 SekundenSolitude is the practice room. It's where the training happens. Whether you call it meditation or just sitting quietly with yourself for a while.
Kapitel 19: Conclusion
1:46:081 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 8 SekundenNow, there's a modern wrinkle to all of this that's worth being honest about because we're living through a period in
1:46:151 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 15 Sekundenwhich being alone and being truly alone are becoming different things.
1:46:211 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 21 SekundenYou can be physically by yourself in a room and never actually experience solitude because your phone is offering
1:46:281 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 28 Sekundenyou an endless stream of other minds to engage with. texts, feeds, notifications,
1:46:361 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 36 Sekundenthe low hum of digital connection that fills the space where quiet used to be.
1:46:421 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 42 SekundenAnd there's nothing wrong with that in itself. Social connection is a genuine human need, and digital tools can serve it.
1:46:521 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 52 SekundenBut the question the research raises, and it's a question more than an answer at this point, is whether constant
1:46:581 Stunde, 46 Minuten, 58 Sekundenlow-grade digital contact is giving us the benefits of social connection or just preventing us from ever sitting
1:47:061 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 6 Sekundenwith ourselves long enough to develop the capacity for solitude. Sherry Turkl, the MIT sociologist who has been
1:47:151 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 15 Sekundenstudying the relationship between technology and selfhood since the 1980s, makes a careful argument about this.
1:47:231 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 23 SekundenShe's not a technophobe, and she's not saying screens are destroying us. What she's saying is subtler. She suggests
1:47:321 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 32 Sekundenthat when we reach for the phone, every time we feel the first flicker of boredom or discomfort, we're interrupting a process. the process of
1:47:411 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 41 Sekundenlearning to be alone with our own thoughts.
1:47:451 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 45 SekundenThe process that Winnott described as a developmental achievement and that the neuroscience now shows involves the
1:47:521 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 52 Sekundendefault mode network settling into a less reactive, more integrative pattern.
1:47:591 Stunde, 47 Minuten, 59 SekundenTurkl calls it the flight from conversation, but it might be more accurate to call it the flight from the inner conversation,
1:48:071 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 7 Sekundenthe one you have with yourself when there's nothing else to respond to. And this connects back to something Reed
1:48:141 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 14 SekundenLarson found in his experience sampling work with adolescence. Remember, he showed that teenagers who spent moderate
1:48:221 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 22 Sekundenamounts of time alone voluntarily showed better emotional adjustment than those who spent either too much or too little.
1:48:311 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 31 SekundenThe key word was voluntary.
1:48:341 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 34 SekundenChosen solitude in manageable doses seemed to help young people develop something, a tolerance for their own
1:48:421 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 42 Sekundeninner life, a familiarity with the rhythms of their own thinking. And you can see how constant digital stimulation
1:48:501 Stunde, 48 Minuten, 50 Sekundenmight interfere with that. Not dramatically, not catastrophically, but quietly by never letting the silence last long enough to become comfortable.
1:49:011 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 1 SekundeThis isn't a moral argument. It's a developmental one.
1:49:051 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 5 SekundenThe capacity for solitude seems to require at some point actual solitude.
1:49:121 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 12 SekundenNot permanent withdrawal, not a rejection of connection, just enough uninterrupted time with your own mind
1:49:191 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 19 Sekundenthat the default mode network can do what it does, which is wander and connect and process. And sometimes when the conditions are right, create.
1:49:331 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 33 SekundenThe research on creativity and solitude, the work that traces back to schooler and Smallwood studies on mind wandering
1:49:421 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 42 Sekundensuggests that some of the most useful cognitive work happens precisely when we're not trying to do anything. When
1:49:501 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 50 Sekundenthe mind is left to its own devices, it doesn't just idle. It recombines. It finds connections between things that
1:49:591 Stunde, 49 Minuten, 59 Sekundenseemed unrelated. It rehearses possible futures. It digests the past. And all of
1:50:061 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 6 Sekundenthat requires a kind of cognitive space that constant input, however pleasant, tends to fill. So here's the picture, as
1:50:151 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 15 Sekundenhonestly as the evidence allows us to draw it. Being alone is not one thing.
1:50:221 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 22 SekundenIt never has been. It's a space. And what happens in that space depends on what you bring to it. Bring a mind
1:50:311 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 31 Sekundenthat's been starved of connection, that's running Kaciopo's hypervigilance program, scanning every silence for threat, and the space will feel hostile.
1:50:431 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 43 SekundenBring a mind that's been gently trained through secure relationships, through practice, through the slow accumulation
1:50:511 Stunde, 50 Minuten, 51 Sekundenof experience with its own inner weather. And the same space becomes something else, not paradise, not bliss,
1:51:011 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 1 Sekundejust a room with someone in it you know reasonably well and don't mind spending time with. And the beautiful thing, the
1:51:101 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 10 Sekundengenuinely hopeful thing is that the research suggests movement between those two experiences is possible at almost
1:51:191 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 19 Sekundenany point in a life. The neural pathways aren't set in stone. The attachment patterns, while sticky, aren't destiny.
1:51:291 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 29 Sekundenthe default mode network, that restless self-reerential system that Rashel stumbled onto and Buckner mapped and
1:51:371 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 37 SekundenBrewer watched change in meditators. It responds to experience. It learns
1:51:441 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 44 Sekundenslowly, sometimes frustratingly, slowly, but it learns. The mind that finds
1:51:511 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 51 Sekundensilence unbearable at 25 may find it nourishing at 40.
1:51:561 Stunde, 51 Minuten, 56 SekundenNot because the silence changed, but because the mind did.
1:52:021 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 2 SekundenBecause somewhere along the way, through relationships, through reflection, through the ordinary accumulation of
1:52:111 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 11 Sekundenself-nowledge that comes from paying attention to your own life, the inner landscape became a little more familiar,
1:52:191 Stunde, 52 Minuten, 19 Sekundena little less threatening, a little more like home.
